+# History & Statistics
+
+## Summary
+
+Audited `MonoHistory.jsx`, `MonoStatistics.jsx`, and `hooks/useGameHistory.js` end-to-end across guest, signed-in, desktop, mobile-web, and Capacitor contexts. The screens are functional and the unit-tested scoring math is sound, but the **derived stats layer has two real correctness bugs**: a substring-based winner resolver that mis-attributes wins/losses when one team name is a prefix of another, and cross-sport margin math that compares incomparable units (cricket runs vs sets-won vs goals) inside the same "Closest / Biggest / Avg margin" insights and the History "Close games" filter. There is also a latent crash path in `useGameHistory` for malformed legacy records, several stale-data / non-reactive issues, and a sport-scoping mislabel where quick-only insights sit next to tournament-inclusive totals with no qualifier.
+
+Severity tally: critical 1, high 4, medium 5, low 6.
+
+**Verified working (no defect):** the Rematch action prefills teams correctly — `rematchEntry` navigates to `/${entry.sport}/quick` with `state.teams` (`MonoHistory.jsx:433-440`), and `MonoQuickMatch` reads `location.state?.teams` into `team1Name`/`team2Name` initial state (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:299-301`). Rematch is also correctly offered only for quick-match entries (which carry a `sport` id) and hidden for tournament/legacy rows.
+
+### [CRITICAL] `useGameHistory` crashes the History screen on a malformed legacy record
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/hooks/useGameHistory.js:30` (`const stats = getStats();`) → `src/utils/universalStorage.js:85-93` (`record.participants.forEach(...)` and `record.finalScores[name]`).
+- **What's wrong:** `getStats()` is called unconditionally in the hook body on every render. It does `record.participants.forEach(...)` with no guard. If any `gs_history` record is missing `participants` (or `finalScores`) — old/corrupted/partially-migrated data — this throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'forEach')`, which propagates out of `useGameHistory()` and crashes `MonoHistory` (the hook is called at the top of the component, line 232). Note the computed `stats` value is **not even consumed** by MonoHistory, so the crash is from dead work. The component's own `legacyEntries` mapping defensively uses `record.participants || []` (`MonoHistory.jsx:287`), proving the authors know these records can be malformed — but `getStats` does not.
+- **User impact:** A single bad legacy record turns the entire History page into a white screen / error boundary. Affects returning users with old data, and anyone whose storage was hand-edited or partially written during a quota failure.
+- **Suggested direction:** Guard `getStats` (`record.participants?.forEach`, default `finalScores` to `{}`), and/or don't call `getStats()` in `useGameHistory` when the consumer doesn't use it (make it lazy like `getStatsForGame`).
+- **Confidence:** medium (crash is conditional on malformed data; the unguarded `.forEach` and unconditional call are certain).
+
+### [HIGH] Substring winner resolver mis-attributes wins when one team name is a prefix of another
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoStatistics.jsx:49-59` (`resolveWinner`), used by `buildQuickTeamRows` (line 97) and `buildQuickInsights` (lines 144, 166-167).
+- **What's wrong:** The stored `winner` is always the **exact** team name string (or `'Tie'`/`'Draw'`) — verified in `MonoQuickMatch.jsx` `getWinnerName` (209-211), `finishCricketMatch` (932-934), and `persistQuickMatch` normalization (600). So `===` is the correct comparison. Instead the code does `winnerText.includes(team1)` / `winnerText.includes(team2)` (lines 54-55). When team1's name is a substring of the actual winner's name and team1 *lost*, the first `includes` matches the wrong team. Example: team1="India", team2="India B", stored winner="India B" → `"india b".includes("india")` is true → returns `match.team1` ("India"). The loser is recorded as the winner.
+- **User impact:** Wrong W / L / Win% in the **Quick team form table** (`buildQuickTeamRows`) and wrong "Top team / Current streak / Last 5 form / Biggest win" insight cards. Silent wrong results a user can notice ("it says India won but India B won"). Affects any user with prefix-overlapping team names (common: "Team A"/"Team A2", "Raj"/"Rajesh", "India"/"India B").
+- **Suggested direction:** Compare exactly: `if (match.winner === match.team1) return match.team1; if (match.winner === match.team2) return match.team2;` then fall back to score, then `'Draw'`. History already does this correctly via `normalizeNonTeamWinner` + the stored name.
+- **Confidence:** high.
+
+### [HIGH] Cross-sport margins compare incomparable units (runs vs sets-won vs goals vs points)
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoStatistics.jsx:141` (`Math.abs(score.score1 - score.score2)`), insights at 152-159, 172, 178-179, 183; History "Close games" filter at `MonoHistory.jsx:113-128` + 660-figure margin `<= 2`.
+- **What's wrong:** For best-of sets quick matches, stored `score1`/`score2` are **sets won** (e.g. `3 - 1`, margin 2) — confirmed in `getBestOfResultScore` (`utils/quickMatchSets.js:64-75`, `score1: setsWon1`). For cricket they're **runs** (e.g. `250 - 248`, margin 2). For goals sports they're **goals** (e.g. `3 - 1`). For single-set sports they're **points** (e.g. `25 - 23`). The Overview insight cards "Closest match", "Biggest win", and "Avg margin" pool all of these into one number, and History's "Close games" filter flags `margin <= 2` across all of them. A volleyball best-of-5 ending 3-1 (margin 2 sets = a blowout) is reported "close", while a cricket nail-biter and a basketball game are averaged together into a meaningless "avg margin".
+- **User impact:** "Biggest win" and "Avg margin" insights are numerically nonsensical for any mixed-sport history; "Close games" filter mislabels lopsided set results as close and vice-versa. Affects every multi-sport user (the app's core "any game" promise).
+- **Suggested direction:** Normalize margin per sport/engine (e.g. for sets use point differential of the deciding set, or compute margin within a single sport only), or scope these insights per-sport, or label them with units. At minimum exclude sets-won pseudo-scores from margin math.
+- **Confidence:** high.
+
+### [HIGH] Overview insight cards are quick-match-only but sit beside tournament-inclusive totals with no qualifier
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoStatistics.jsx:330` (`buildQuickInsights(quickMatches)`), rendered 380-388; totals `totalTournaments`/`totalMatches`/`totalTeams` include tournaments (323-329, 374-376).
+- **What's wrong:** All nine InsightCards ("Top team", "Last 5 form", "Current streak", "Head-to-head", "Closest match", "Biggest win", "Most played sport", "Draw rate", "Avg margin") are computed **only** from `quickMatches`. Tournament results contribute nothing. But the three big StatCards directly above them ("Tournaments / Matches / Teams") **do** include tournament data. A user who only ran tournaments sees "Matches: 40, Teams: 8" next to "Top team: No winner yet", "Current streak: No active streak", "Most played sport: No sport yet" — the insights look broken/empty even though the user has lots of data.
+- **User impact:** Misleading: stats appear empty/wrong for tournament-only users; quick-only users get the opposite. No label says these insights are quick-match-only. Affects all tournament-primary users and any signed-in user whose history skews to tournaments.
+- **Suggested direction:** Either include tournament matches in the insight derivation, or clearly label the insight block "Quick matches" and gate it on `quickMatches.length > 0`.
+- **Confidence:** high.
+
+### [HIGH] `buildTournamentEntries` aggregates over ALL sports' tournaments on every History mount (no memo, O(sports x tournaments x matches))
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoHistory.jsx:192-228` + call at 253; `MonoStatistics.jsx:233-251` mirrors it.
+- **What's wrong:** On mount, History iterates all 14 sports, calls `loadSportTournaments(storageKey)` (which `JSON.parse`s each sport's full tournament blob AND can re-`saveData` on migration — `storage.js:216-232`), then flattens every match of every tournament and resolves names. This is synchronous in a `useEffect` with no chunking/memo. For a power user with many tournaments across sports, this is a visible jank/blocking parse on every navigation to History and Statistics. `loadSportTournaments` also performs a write-back during this read path if migration triggers, multiplying cost.
+- **User impact:** Slow/janky History & Statistics open for users with large datasets, especially on low-end Android (Capacitor) where JSON.parse of large blobs blocks the UI thread. This is the "large-history performance" concern in scope.
+- **Suggested direction:** Memoize/cache the tournament aggregation, parse lazily per active tab (Statistics already tabs by sport), and move the migration write-back out of the read hot path.
+- **Confidence:** medium (correctness is fine; cost scales with data the user may not have).
+
+### [MEDIUM] Quick-match id collisions (`Date.now()`) can delete two matches at once and break dedupe
+
+- **Evidence:** id assigned as `Date.now()` in `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1271` and `:936`; History delete filters by `qm.id !== id` (`MonoHistory.jsx:349`), Statistics by `qm.id !== id` (`MonoStatistics.jsx:257`); `saveQuickMatch` upsert matches by `m.id === match.id` (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:32`).
+- **What's wrong:** Two matches finalized within the same millisecond get the same numeric id. Deleting one then removes both (`filter` drops all with that id), and the upsert in `saveQuickMatch` would overwrite rather than append. React `key={qm.id}` would also collide (duplicate-key warning, render glitches).
+- **User impact:** Rare data loss (deleting one quick match silently deletes another) and list rendering warnings. Low probability but real, and the impact is silent loss.
+- **Suggested direction:** Use a collision-resistant id (`crypto.randomUUID()` or `Date.now() + '-' + counter`).
+- **Confidence:** medium (mechanism certain; collision requires two saves in the same ms).
+
+### [MEDIUM] History data is non-reactive: deleting a quick match in Statistics doesn't update History (and vice-versa) until remount
+
+- **Evidence:** Both screens load into local state once in `useEffect([])` (`MonoHistory.jsx:246-254`, `MonoStatistics.jsx:222-254`) and write to localStorage on mutation, but neither re-reads on focus/visibility or listens to `storage` events.
+- **What's wrong:** Quick matches and tournaments live in shared localStorage but each screen snapshots them at mount. The danger dialogs even say "removed from Statistics and History" (`MonoStatistics.jsx:473`), but if both screens are alive (e.g. back/forward navigation that keeps them mounted, or a freshly finished match) the other screen shows stale counts/rows until it remounts. A match completed in MonoQuickMatch won't appear in an already-open History.
+- **User impact:** Confusing stale counts ("All matches: 5" but 6 rows elsewhere), deleted items reappearing after navigating back, newly-finished matches missing. Affects normal navigation, more visible on mobile where screens are kept warm.
+- **Suggested direction:** Re-read on route focus (`useEffect` keyed on location, or a `visibilitychange`/`storage` listener), or centralize quick-match state in a shared hook/store.
+- **Confidence:** medium.
+
+### [MEDIUM] History "Close games" filter parses the display-score string, so sets results are mis-flagged as close
+
+- **Evidence:** `getScoreMargin`/`splitScoreSides`/`getLeadingNumber` (`MonoHistory.jsx:81-119`), used by `matchesResultFilter` for `RESULT_CLOSE` (125-128), threshold `margin <= 2`.
+- **What's wrong:** The "Close games" filter derives margin by string-parsing `entry.score` rather than structured fields. For a tournament/quick **sets** match the score string is sets-won, e.g. `"3 - 1"`, so `getScoreMargin` returns 2 → flagged "close" even though 3-1 is a comfortable best-of-5 win. Cricket strings like `"120/5 vs 118/7"` parse to 120 vs 118 (margin 2, genuinely close), so the same threshold means completely different things depending on the row's sport. This is the History-side manifestation of the cross-sport units defect, and because it works off the display string it silently sweeps in tournament and legacy rows too.
+- **User impact:** "Close games" filter shows lopsided set wins as close and is not comparable across sports; users can't trust the filter.
+- **Suggested direction:** Compute margin from structured score fields with sport/engine awareness, not the rendered display string.
+- **Confidence:** medium.
+
+### [MEDIUM] `stats` recomputed (and localStorage re-read) on every render of any History consumer
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/hooks/useGameHistory.js:30` — `const stats = getStats();` runs in the hook body, not memoized; `getStats` does `loadHistory()` (full `JSON.parse`) + reduce (`universalStorage.js:80-103`).
+- **What's wrong:** Every render of `MonoHistory` (and any other `useGameHistory` consumer) re-reads and re-parses `gs_history` and recomputes participant stats, even though `MonoHistory` never reads `stats`. Combined with the per-mount tournament aggregation this adds avoidable work.
+- **User impact:** Minor perf / battery; negligible for small data, additive for large.
+- **Suggested direction:** Memoize with `useMemo` keyed on `history`, or expose `stats` lazily.
+- **Confidence:** high (the unconditional call is certain; impact is small).
+
+### [MEDIUM] `getMatchDate`/sort treat invalid or missing dates as epoch/NaN inconsistently, scrambling order
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoStatistics.jsx:12-13` (`new Date(... || 0)` → epoch 1970 for missing), `:226` sort with `new Date(...)` (NaN if all date fields absent → NaN subtraction → unstable sort); History uses `toTimestamp` returning `0` for invalid (`MonoHistory.jsx:30-33`).
+- **What's wrong:** Two different invalid-date strategies. In Statistics, a match with no date field yields `new Date(0)` = 1970 (sorts to bottom), but a match with a present-but-unparseable date yields `Invalid Date` whose subtraction is `NaN`, making the comparator non-transitive and the sort order undefined. "Recent quick matches", "Last 5 form", "Current streak", and `latestWinner` (`buildQuickInsights:166-167`) all depend on this sort — a single bad date can corrupt the computed streak and form.
+- **User impact:** Wrong "Current streak" / "Last 5 form" / "latest winner" when any record has a malformed date; inconsistent ordering between History and Statistics for the same data.
+- **Suggested direction:** Use the History `toTimestamp` helper everywhere (finite-or-0), and guard the comparator against NaN.
+- **Confidence:** medium.
+
+### [LOW] `isDrawWinner` / streak handle a `'Tie'` value that `resolveWinner` can never return (dead branch)
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoStatistics.jsx:58` (`resolveWinner` only ever returns a team name or `'Draw'`), but `isDrawWinner` checks `'Tie'` (61-63) and streak guards `latestWinner !== 'Draw'` (168) without `'Tie'`.
+- **What's wrong:** For quick matches surfaced through `resolveWinner`, `'Tie'` is unreachable, so the `'Tie'` arm of `isDrawWinner` is dead and the streak `!== 'Draw'` guard is sufficient. However, the *stored* cricket winner is literally `'Tie'` (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:934`); Statistics never reads `match.winner` directly for streaks (it re-derives via `resolveWinner`, which maps equal cricket scores to `'Draw'`), so a tied cricket match shows as "Draw" in derived stats but `'Tie'` in the raw "Recent quick matches" winner label (line 600). Cosmetic inconsistency (Draw vs Tie wording for the same match).
+- **User impact:** Same tied cricket match labelled "Tie" in the recent list but counted under "Draw" elsewhere. Minor wording inconsistency.
+- **Suggested direction:** Normalize tie/draw vocabulary once; let `resolveWinner` preserve the stored `'Tie'`.
+- **Confidence:** high (behavior traced).
+
+### [LOW] No list virtualization — every history row and every quick-match row renders
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoHistory.jsx:719` (`filteredEntries.map`), `MonoStatistics.jsx:587` (`quickMatches.map`).
+- **What's wrong:** All rows render at once. For a user with hundreds/thousands of matches the DOM node count grows unbounded.
+- **User impact:** Scroll jank / high memory on large histories, worst on low-end Android.
+- **Suggested direction:** Virtualize or paginate (e.g. show 50, "load more").
+- **Confidence:** high.
+
+### [LOW] Hardcoded legacy `#0066ff` blue used for winner / Win% / margin colors
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoHistory.jsx:747`, `768`; `MonoStatistics.jsx:600`, `682`, `685`, `854`, `855`.
+- **What's wrong:** The design system (per project memory) declared `#0066ff` dead and replaced by `var(--primary)` green. History/Statistics still hardcode `#0066ff` for winner text, Win%, and positive-margin colors, so these screens render in the old blue, off-brand vs the rest of the app.
+- **User impact:** Visual inconsistency — blue accents on History/Statistics while the app is green.
+- **Suggested direction:** Replace with `var(--primary)` / `var(--se-color-action)` tokens (Statistics already uses `var(--se-color-action)` for the sport icon at line 398, proving the token exists).
+- **Confidence:** high.
+
+### [LOW] Undo/clear flows assume `saveData` succeeds; on quota failure the UI shows "restored" while storage is unchanged
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoHistory.jsx:375-381` (`undoDeleteQuickMatch`), `410-418` (`undoClearMutableHistory`), `MonoStatistics.jsx:282-320`. All call `saveData(...)` and unconditionally set status text "restored" / "cleared".
+- **What's wrong:** `saveData` returns `false` on quota/private-mode failure (`storage.js:133-140`) but every undo/restore/clear path ignores the return value and reports success. The delete path in MonoQuickMatch DOES check (`persistQuickMatch`, 605-608); these screens don't.
+- **User impact:** In Safari private mode or near-quota, a user is told "History restored" / "Quick stats cleared" while the on-disk state silently diverges from the displayed state; on next mount the data reverts unexpectedly.
+- **Suggested direction:** Check `saveData` return and surface a failure status, mirroring `persistQuickMatch`.
+- **Confidence:** medium.
+
+### [LOW] Share-from-history builds text from possibly-`undefined` fields; legacy/tournament entries lack `sport`, so Rematch is correctly hidden but Share text can read oddly
+
+- **Evidence:** `buildEntryShareText` (`MonoHistory.jsx:132-135`) uses `entry.team1`/`team2`/`score`; legacy entries set team1/team2 to `'Player 1'`/`'Player 2'` fallback (291-298), tournament entries always have names. `rematchEntry` guards on `entry.sport` (433-434) and the Rematch button only renders for `source === 'quick'` (802) — correct.
+- **What's wrong:** Minor: share text for a legacy entry is `"<tournamentName> - <scores> - <winner> won"` which is fine, but `winnerLabel` of a missing winner yields `"-- "` producing `"... - -- "`. Not harmful but unpolished. Rematch correctly unavailable for tournament/legacy (no `sport`).
+- **User impact:** Occasional ugly share string for entries with no winner; no functional break.
+- **Suggested direction:** Trim/guard the share template when winner is unknown.
+- **Confidence:** medium.
+
+### [LOW] `sportFilter` matches by display name, not id — two sports with same display name (or renamed registry) would merge
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoHistory.jsx:315` (`entry.sportName === sportFilter`), options built from `entry.sportName` (324-327).
+- **What's wrong:** Filtering keys on the human name rather than the stable sport id. Quick entries derive `sportName` from `getSportById(qm.sport)?.name` but fall back to the raw `qm.sport` id when unknown (line 272), so an unknown/old sport id shows as a raw slug option and a registry rename would orphan old rows under a different label.
+- **User impact:** Edge-case: matches saved under a since-renamed or removed sport appear under a raw-id label and can't be cleanly filtered with current ones.
+- **Suggested direction:** Filter on `entry.sport` (id) where available; use name only for display.
+- **Confidence:** medium.
