+# Mobile / Android / Capacitor (code-audited)
+
+## Summary
+
+Audited the Capacitor 8 native layer of Score Easy — `src/mobile/{backButton,deepLinks,share,platform,lifecycle,haptics,nativeChrome}.js`, `capacitor.config.ts`, Android manifest, iOS entitlements/Info.plist, safe-area CSS, and the scoring-route back-button/deep-link wiring in `src/designs/design1-mono/index.jsx`. The native modules themselves are well-structured and well-tested, but several of them are bypassed or unused by the actual scoring screens: the dedicated `haptics.js` native module is ignored by all five live-scoring engines (they call `navigator.vibrate` directly, which is a no-op on iOS WKWebView), and `lifecycle.js` (`addAppStateChangeListener`) is dead code, leaving in-progress tournament scoring with no flush-on-background and a real data-loss window. Remaining findings are UX/polish (misleading exit and share-cancel messaging) plus a set of behaviours that are only fully confirmable on a physical device/emulator (safe-area under the notch, soft-keyboard occlusion, App Links server verification). All findings below were verified against source; the StatusBar style and the back-button history machinery were checked and found correct (no defect).
+
+Severity tally: critical 0 · high 1 · medium 5 · low 3
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] In-progress tournament scoring is not persisted per point and has no flush-on-background (data-loss window)
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoSetsLiveScore.jsx:193` (`addPoint`) mutates React state and sets `setHasChanges(true)` but never writes to storage; the only persistence is the explicit `saveDraft()` (`:294`, calls `saveSportTournament` at `:311`) and the match-complete path. There is no autosave `useEffect` keyed to score changes (the only effects are Convex save on completion `:66` and a desktop keyboard handler `:380`). The same pattern holds for the other tournament engines (`MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx`, `MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx`). The native lifecycle hook that could flush on background — `src/mobile/lifecycle.js:4` `addAppStateChangeListener` — is **never imported anywhere in `src/`** (dead code).
+- **What's wrong:** While a user scores a tournament match, every point lives only in component memory until they tap "save draft" or finish the match. On Android, a backgrounded WebView app can be killed by the OS at any time. With no `appStateChange` listener wired in, there is no opportunity to flush the in-progress `sets/history/currentSet` to `localStorage` before the process dies.
+- **User impact:** A signed-in or guest user mid-tournament-match who switches apps (takes a call, checks a message) can return to find the app relaunched and all unsaved points gone, reverting to the last explicit draft save (or to zero if none). Quick Match is NOT affected — it autosaves every point (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx` draft effect ~470-499) — so this is specifically a tournament-scoring gap.
+- **Suggested direction:** Either (a) add a score-change `useEffect` in the tournament engines that debounce-writes `draftState` to `saveSportTournament`, mirroring Quick Match, and/or (b) wire `addAppStateChangeListener` at app root to flush the active scorer's draft when `appStateChange` reports `isActive === false`.
+- **Confidence:** medium (the no-per-point-persistence is code-confirmed; the OS-kill trigger and exact loss boundary are device-confirmable).
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Live-scoring engines bypass the native Haptics module — haptics are dead on iOS
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoSetsLiveScore.jsx:16`, `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:24`, `MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:20`, `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:19`, `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:20` each define a local `triggerHaptic` that calls `navigator.vibrate(...)` directly. None import `src/mobile/haptics.js`. Only `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:22-28` uses the real native module (`scoreImpact`/`correctionImpact`). These engines back all tournament scoring (`MonoTournamentLiveScore.jsx:6-10` renders all five) and the dedicated cricket/tennis routes.
+- **What's wrong:** iOS WKWebView does not implement the Web Vibration API, so `navigator.vibrate` is a silent no-op there. The app ships a correct `haptics.js` that calls the `@capacitor/haptics` plugin with a `navigator.vibrate` web fallback — but the scoring engines don't use it. (Android WebView does support `navigator.vibrate` and `@capacitor/haptics` is installed, so Android likely still vibrates; the gap is iOS-specific.)
+- **User impact:** On iOS native, scoring a point / winning a set / winning a match produces no haptic feedback during tournament play and on the cricket/tennis routes, even though the same actions buzz correctly in Quick Match. Inconsistent, degraded tactile feedback.
+- **Suggested direction:** Replace the per-component `triggerHaptic` with imports from `src/mobile/haptics.js` (`scoreImpact`/`correctionImpact`/`endMatchImpact`), as `MonoQuickMatch` already does.
+- **Confidence:** high (iOS no-Vibration-API is well established; the bypass is direct in source).
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Scoring screens have no top safe-area inset while the only inset-bearing chrome is hidden (notch collision under viewport-fit=cover)
+
+- **Evidence:** `index.html:8` sets `viewport-fit=cover`, so content extends under the notch/Dynamic Island. The top safe-area padding lives only on `.global-nav` (`src/designs/design1-mono/index.jsx:796` `padding: env(safe-area-inset-top,0px) 16px 0`) and the mobile menu button (`:805`). On scoring routes the global nav is hidden (`index.jsx:347` `showBottomNav = !isProtectedScoringPath`). The scoring components instead use hardcoded top padding (`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:490` / `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:551` `paddingTop:'80px'`) with no `env(safe-area-inset-top)` anywhere under `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/`.
+- **What's wrong:** With `viewport-fit=cover` and the inset-bearing nav removed, the scoring header/back affordance can render under the status bar / notch / Dynamic Island on notched devices, since the fixed 80px is not derived from the actual inset.
+- **User impact:** On iPhones with a notch/Dynamic Island (and some Android cutouts), the top of the scoring UI (back button, score header) may be partially obscured or hard to tap. Affects Android/iOS native and notched mobile-web.
+- **Suggested direction:** Add `env(safe-area-inset-top)` to the top padding of the scoring layouts (e.g. `max(80px, env(safe-area-inset-top))` or a wrapper inset), since the global nav inset no longer applies on these routes.
+- **Confidence:** medium (CSS gap is code-confirmed; exact visual overlap is device-confirmable per the existing `docs/mobile/native-device-verification-runbook.md`).
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Cancelling the native/web share sheet is reported as "Share is not available on this device"
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/mobile/share.js:14-16` and `:23-26` treat ANY rejection from `Share.share` / `navigator.share` as failure (`method:'native-failed'` / `'web-share-failed'`, `shared:false`). `src/utils/quickMatchResult.js:12` then maps any `shared:false` to `'Share is not available on this device.'`, shown via `setShareStatus`/`setHistoryStatus` in `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1322` and `MonoHistory.jsx:427`.
+- **What's wrong:** On iOS/Android the native share sheet rejects with an `AbortError` when the user simply taps "Cancel"/dismisses it; `navigator.share` does the same on web. The code cannot distinguish "user cancelled" from "sharing unsupported," so a normal cancel surfaces a false "not available on this device" message. (`share.test.js` does not cover the cancel/reject path, so the suite stays green.)
+- **User impact:** A user who opens the share sheet and changes their mind is told their device can't share — confusing and slightly alarming, and it contradicts the share sheet they just saw.
+- **Suggested direction:** Detect `AbortError` (and empty `error.message`) in the catch blocks and return a distinct `method:'cancelled'` that maps to no message (or a neutral one), reserving "not available" for the true `unsupported` branch.
+- **Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Back-button exit prompt warns of "unsaved scoring progress" on the Quick Match setup and result screens
+
+- **Evidence:** The protected-route test matches any path segment `quick` (`src/mobile/backButton.js:7` and `index.jsx:157`). `MonoQuickMatch` serves `setup | scoring | result` phases all on the same `/:sport/quick` URL (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:273`, `:1372`, `:2191`). The native guard always uses the message "Leave this page? Your unsaved scoring progress may be lost." (`backButton.js:31`) and the in-app dialog uses `SCORING_EXIT_MESSAGE` regardless of phase.
+- **What's wrong:** The guard is purely route-based, not phase-aware. During **setup** (before any point is scored) and during **result** (after the match has already been saved), pressing hardware/browser back still raises a "unsaved scoring progress may be lost" confirmation.
+- **User impact:** False-alarm friction: a user who opens match setup, or who has already saved and is viewing the result, is warned about losing scoring progress that either doesn't exist yet or is already saved. Affects every platform; most jarring on Android hardware-back.
+- **Suggested direction:** Gate the confirmation on actual unsaved state (e.g. only prompt when phase === 'scoring' with `hasChanges`), or expose phase to the guard rather than keying solely on the `quick` segment.
+- **Confidence:** high (phase model and route matcher are both code-confirmed).
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] No Keyboard plugin / keyboard-resize handling for text inputs in setup
+
+- **Evidence:** `@capacitor/keyboard` is not in `package.json` dependencies (only `app`, `core`, `haptics`, `share`, `splash-screen`, `status-bar`), and `capacitor.config.ts` has no `Keyboard` plugin block or resize config. Team-name `<input>`s appear in the Quick Match / tournament setup flows; `MonoTournamentSetup.jsx:22` positions a sticky action bar at `calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom,0px) + 76px)`.
+- **What's wrong:** Without the Keyboard plugin and a resize strategy, the Android soft keyboard can overlay bottom-anchored inputs/action bars with no automatic scroll-into-view or webview resize. The native-device runbook explicitly lists "Keyboard safety" as a check, confirming it's an open concern.
+- **User impact:** On Android native, focusing a team-name field near the bottom of the screen may hide the field or the primary action button behind the keyboard. Affects Android native primarily.
+- **Suggested direction:** Add `@capacitor/keyboard` with `resize: 'body'` (or `native`) and ensure focused inputs scroll into view; verify against the runbook keyboard-safety step.
+- **Confidence:** medium (plugin absence is code-confirmed; occlusion severity is device-confirmable).
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] iOS `UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance = true` can override the plugin-set status-bar style
+
+- **Evidence:** `ios/App/App/Info.plist:48-49` sets `UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance` to `true`; `src/mobile/nativeChrome.js:12` sets the style via `StatusBar.setStyle({ style: Style.Light })`.
+- **What's wrong:** When view-controller-based appearance is enabled, the active `UIViewController` controls the status-bar style and can override the value the Capacitor StatusBar plugin sets from JS, so `setStyle` may not take effect reliably on iOS. (Note: `Style.Light` itself is correct here — per `@capacitor/status-bar` it means "dark text for light backgrounds," matching the `#fafafa` chrome. Not a bug.)
+- **User impact:** Potential mismatched status-bar text color on iOS (light text over the near-white `#fafafa` bar would be unreadable) in some navigation states. iOS native only.
+- **Suggested direction:** Confirm on-device; if the style doesn't stick, set `UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance` to `false` or manage style via the view controller.
+- **Confidence:** low (a known Capacitor caveat; needs device confirmation).
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] App Links / Universal Links depend on server-hosted association files not present in this repo
+
+- **Evidence:** `android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml:26-33` declares an `autoVerify="true"` https intent-filter for `scoreeasy.app`; `ios/App/App/App.entitlements:5-8` declares `applinks:scoreeasy.app`. No `.well-known/assetlinks.json` or `apple-app-site-association` exists anywhere in the repo.
+- **What's wrong:** Android App Links verification requires `https://scoreeasy.app/.well-known/assetlinks.json`; iOS Universal Links require `apple-app-site-association`. These are served from the domain (often a separate web project), so their absence here is expected rather than a code defect — but it is the single point that determines whether deep links open the app vs. the browser.
+- **User impact:** If the association files are missing/misconfigured on the live domain, tapping a `scoreeasy.app` link opens the browser instead of the installed app, and `appUrlOpen`/`getLaunchUrl` deep-link routing (`src/mobile/deepLinks.js`) never fires. Android/iOS native.
+- **Suggested direction:** Verify both files are deployed and correct on `scoreeasy.app` (fingerprints / team-id + bundle-id); track via the native-device verification runbook. Informational for this codebase.
+- **Confidence:** medium (manifest/entitlement state is code-confirmed; server file state is deployment/device-confirmable).
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Share payloads never include a deep-link URL back to the match
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1317-1321` and `MonoHistory.jsx:422-426` call `shareText({ title, text, dialogTitle })` with no `url`. `share.js:8` accepts `url` and `pathFromAppUrl`/deep-link routing exists to handle `https://scoreeasy.app/...` links.
+- **What's wrong:** Shared results are plain text with no link back to the app or a viewable match, despite the deep-link infrastructure being in place.
+- **User impact:** Recipients of a shared score have no tap-through into Score Easy; a missed engagement/growth path, not a functional defect.
+- **Suggested direction:** Consider including a `url` (e.g. a `scoreeasy.app` link) so shares deep-link into the app on recipients' devices.
+- **Confidence:** high (call sites confirmed).
+
+---
+
+## Notes on items checked and found NOT defective
+
+- **StatusBar style (`Style.Light` on `#fafafa`)** — correct. `@capacitor/status-bar` defines `Light = "dark text for light backgrounds"` (`node_modules/@capacitor/status-bar/dist/esm/definitions.d.ts:54-58`), matching the light chrome. Earlier suspicion of inverted/invisible text was a false positive.
+- **Browser-back / popstate machinery on scoring routes** (`index.jsx:1093-1192`) — traced; history-index bookkeeping, fallback navigation, and native-guard integration are coherent and covered by passing tests. No defect found.
+- **Native back-button registered only on protected routes** — by design. When no `backButton` listener is registered, Capacitor's default does `history.back()` (works with React Router) or exits when history is empty. The only minor consequence is that hardware-back on the root screen exits immediately with no "press back again" confirmation; not treated as a defect.
+- **Deep-link handler cleanup / cold-launch race** (`deepLinks.js`) — guards against post-cleanup navigation and unregisters late-resolving listeners; covered by tests. No defect.
