+# Performance & Efficiency
+
+## Summary
+
+The dominant performance defect is the live-scoring timer: `useTimer()` is instantiated *inside* the 2836-line `MonoQuickMatch` orchestrator (line 270), so its 1-second `setElapsed` tick re-renders the **entire** unmemoized component every second for the full duration of *every* match (sets, goals, cricket — not just timed games), recreating all inline handlers and re-rendering the whole scoring UI. Worse, the draft-autosave effect lists `timer.elapsed` in its dependency array (line 499), so each tick also performs a full `JSON.stringify` of the complete match state (scores + every undo-history array) and a synchronous `localStorage.setItem` — a continuous 1 Hz write that is pure waste because elapsed time is already recoverable from `startedAt`. The orchestrator and `MonoStatistics` contain zero `useMemo`/`useCallback`/`React.memo`; lazy-loading, haptics, Convex team-search gating, and history-array bounding were checked and are sound.
+
+Severity tally: critical 0 · high 2 · medium 4 · low 3
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Timer tick re-renders the entire 2836-line orchestrator every second
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:270` (`const timer = useTimer()`); `src/hooks/useTimer.js:43-45` (`setInterval` → `setElapsed(prev => prev + 1)` every 1000 ms); `useEffect` at `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:416-423` calls `timer.start()` for *any* `phase === 'scoring'`; clock is displayed at `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:2233` (cricket bar) and `:2527` (sets/goals bar).
+- **What's wrong:** Timer state lives in the orchestrator, which has no memoization (38 `useState`, 0 `useMemo`/`useCallback`/`React.memo`). Every 1 Hz tick re-renders the whole component tree, re-instantiating every inline handler (`addPoint`, `addRuns`, `addGoal`, etc.) and re-rendering the ~600-line scoring JSX. `timer.start()` fires for all engines, so volleyball/badminton/tennis/cricket all incur this even though only a small `<span>` clock actually changes.
+- **User impact:** On low-end Android (Capacitor) a long match (cricket test, multi-set volleyball) sustains a full React reconcile every second for tens of minutes → measurable battery drain and periodic micro-jank, independent of whether the user is scoring. Affects every scoring context.
+- **Suggested direction:** Extract the clock into an isolated `<MatchTimer />` leaf component that owns the tick (or `React.memo` it and pass only `elapsed`), so the 1 Hz update repaints one node instead of the orchestrator. Alternatively compute display time from `startedAt` with a throttled local subscription.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+### [HIGH] Draft autosave re-serializes + writes full match to localStorage every second (timer.elapsed in deps)
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:469-499` — the autosave `useEffect` writes the entire draft object (`scores`, `sets`, `vScoreHistory`, `gScoreHistory`, `cricketHistory`, etc.) via `saveData(...)`, and its dependency array (line 499) includes `timer.elapsed`. `saveData` (`src/utils/storage.js:133-140` → `safeSave` :84) does a synchronous `JSON.stringify` + `localStorage.setItem` with no debounce/throttle. Restore already reconstructs elapsed from `startedAt`/`updatedAt` (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:216-217, 463-464` via `getRestoredTimerElapsed`).
+- **What's wrong:** Because `timer.elapsed` changes every second, the effect fires every second even when no point is scored, re-stringifying and persisting the whole match state on the main thread. The dependency conflates "a second passed" with "match state changed." The `timerElapsed` field (line 495) is the only thing that actually changed, and it is redundant with `startedAt`.
+- **User impact:** Continuous synchronous serialization + storage write each second for the entire match. On large states (cricket test with long `cricketHistory`, or sets history up to 100 snapshots) this is a recurring main-thread stall and flash-write churn — wasteful battery/IO on mobile, and a compounding cost with the re-render in the finding above.
+- **Suggested direction:** Remove `timer.elapsed` from the dependency array; persist only on genuine score/state changes and store `startedAt` (already present) so elapsed is derived on restore. Optionally debounce `saveData` for the draft key.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] No vendor code-splitting — 264 KB (~86 KB gzip) single index chunk on first paint
+
+- **Evidence:** `vite.config.js` has no `build.rollupOptions.output.manualChunks`; `dist/assets/index-D8BFcN1l.js` = 264 KB raw / 85.9 KB gzip and contains React-DOM, react-router, Clerk, Convex, and scheduler together (grep hits confirmed in the chunk).
+- **What's wrong:** All core vendor libraries are bundled into one eager chunk that must download + parse before first interactive paint, including for brand-new guests who only see the marketing landing.
+- **User impact:** Slower first contentful/interactive paint on cold load, especially on slow mobile networks; any vendor version bump busts the whole 86 KB cache entry instead of a stable vendor chunk.
+- **Suggested direction:** Add `manualChunks` to split `react`/`react-dom`/`react-router`, `@clerk/*`, and `convex` into separately-cached vendor chunks; consider deferring Clerk/Convex init off the guest landing path.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+### [MEDIUM] MonoStatistics recomputes all aggregations on every render (no memoization)
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoStatistics.jsx` has 0 `useMemo`/`useCallback`; lines 322-338 run `Object.values(sportsData).reduce(...)` (×2), build a `new Set()` over all teams, call `buildQuickTeamRows(quickMatches)` and `buildQuickInsights(quickMatches)`, and `.filter`/`.map` to build tabs — all in the render body. Component also has multiple `useState` (tab, pendingDelete, pendingClear, statsStatus) that trigger re-renders.
+- **What's wrong:** Every state change (tab switch, opening a delete/clear dialog, status message) re-derives the full cross-sport totals and per-team insight aggregations from scratch.
+- **User impact:** With many saved quick matches/tournaments, interacting with the stats page (switching tabs, deleting an entry) does redundant O(matches) work each time — sluggish on large datasets, more so on mobile.
+- **Suggested direction:** Wrap `totalTournaments`, `totalMatches`, `quickTeamRows`, `quickInsights`, `allTeams`, and `tabs` in `useMemo` keyed on `sportsData`/`quickMatches`.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+### [MEDIUM] Orchestrator handlers and derived values recreated every render
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoQuickMatch.jsx` defines `addPoint` (998), `addRuns` (801), `addWicket` (841), `addGoal` (1180), `undoPoint` (1049), `undoGoal` (1202), `adjustSetScore` (1073), etc. as plain consts with no `useCallback`; derived arrays `sortedTeam1`/`sortedTeam2` (333-334) re-`.slice().sort()` on every render.
+- **What's wrong:** Combined with the 1 Hz timer re-render, every handler closure and the sorted-suggestion arrays are rebuilt each second. Children receiving these as props can't bail out (no `React.memo` anywhere).
+- **User impact:** Amplifies the timer-tick cost; mostly mitigated today because scoring UI is inline (no memoized children to break), but it makes any future extraction of `<Timer/>`/scoreboard ineffective until handlers are stabilized.
+- **Suggested direction:** Once the timer is isolated, wrap score handlers in `useCallback` and memoize `sortedTeam1/2` so the still-mounted scoreboard doesn't reconcile on tick.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+### [MEDIUM] Single shared `lastClickRef` debounce blocks legitimate simultaneous taps
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:288` (`lastClickRef`), checked at `addPoint:1000-1002`, `addRuns:802-803`, `addWicket:842-844` — a global 150 ms gate across *all* scoring buttons.
+- **What's wrong:** The debounce is shared, not per-button. A referee scoring rapidly (e.g. cricket: tap "wicket" then immediately "+1 run" for the next ball, or alternating team buttons in fast volleyball rallies) can have the second distinct action silently dropped if within 150 ms.
+- **User impact:** Occasional missed/ignored taps during fast scoring — the user sees no feedback and the score is wrong until they notice. Functional-perf gray area; primarily a fast-scoring UX risk.
+- **Suggested direction:** Scope the debounce per action/button, or lower the window; ensure dropped taps are at least surfaced. (Cross-check with the correctness audit — this overlaps UX.)
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] State setters called inside the `setScores` updater (cricket)
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:814-838` — inside `setScores(prev => {...})`, the updater calls `setInnings`, `setBattingTeam`, and `finishCricketMatch(...)` (lines 821-833). Same pattern in `addWicket` (854+).
+- **What's wrong:** Updater functions should be pure; React may invoke them twice (StrictMode/dev double-render) and side-effecting setters inside them is fragile. Tests pass today, so behavior is currently correct — this is a correctness/maintainability smell, not a measured perf loss.
+- **User impact:** None observed; risk of double-fired transitions under future React behavior changes.
+- **Suggested direction:** Compute the next scores purely, then perform innings/finish transitions in an effect or after the setter returns.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+### [LOW] `requestAnimationFrame(() => setVisible(true))` fade-in pattern in list pages
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoStatistics.jsx:223` (`requestAnimationFrame(() => setVisible(true))` in the load effect); similar visibility-driven fade patterns across mono pages.
+- **What's wrong:** Forces an extra render immediately after mount purely for a fade-in; harmless but a small, avoidable double-render on every navigation to the page.
+- **User impact:** Negligible; one extra commit per page entry.
+- **Suggested direction:** Prefer CSS-only entrance animation to avoid the state-driven re-render.
+- **Confidence:** low
+
+### [LOW] Draft write has no throttle even on genuine score changes
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:473` `saveData(...)` runs synchronously on every dependency change; `storage.js:84` stringifies the full object each time.
+- **What's wrong:** Even after removing `timer.elapsed` from deps (HIGH finding), each scored point still triggers a full-state `JSON.stringify` + `setItem`. For cricket with a long `cricketHistory`, the serialized payload grows over the match and is rewritten in full per ball.
+- **User impact:** Minor main-thread cost per point on long matches; bounded by the 150 ms tap debounce so not a fast-scoring blocker.
+- **Suggested direction:** Debounce/idle-schedule the draft write (e.g. `requestIdleCallback` or a 250-500 ms trailing debounce) so bursts coalesce.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+## Checked — not issues
+
+- **Lazy-loading:** comprehensive and correct — every route component is `lazy()` + a single `Suspense` boundary (`index.jsx:17-45, 1201-1250`); `MonoQuickMatch` ships as its own 68 KB chunk.
+- **Haptics:** `scoreImpact`/`correctionImpact` are async fire-and-forget (`void`) and no-op off-native via `canUseHaptics()` (`mobile/haptics.js`) — no main-thread cost on web.
+- **Convex team-search:** `useQuery(api.teams.search, ... : 'skip')` is correctly `'skip'`-ed unless authenticated + suggestions open + ≥2 chars (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:319-330`), so it does not fire during scoring.
+- **Undo-history growth:** sets history is capped with `.slice(-100)` (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1015`); cricket history entries are tiny primitive objects (`:812, :852`) — no large snapshots accumulate.
+- **MonoHistory:** uses `useMemo` for its 5 derived/filtered lists (`MonoHistory.jsx:256-324`) — already memoized.
