+# Persistence & Data Integrity
+
+## Summary
+
+Score Easy persists everything to `localStorage` under an `se_` prefix through `src/utils/storage.js`, with a quota-aware `safeSave`, a Safari-private-mode memory fallback, and tournament/quick-match format migrations. The core save paths are functional and the quota/private-mode handling is genuinely good. However, the persistence layer has a fragmented schema (three storage systems coexist: `se_*`, `gs_*`, and per-sport keys), and several integrity gaps appear at the seams: a corrupt or wrong-shaped value crashes every list-reading screen with no recovery, an entire dead write-path (`gs_*` sessions/history) feeds live UI that can never populate, in-progress cricket-test drafts pollute completed-match history and statistics, match IDs are `Date.now()` (collision + multi-tab clobber risk), and the universal "Export your data" recovery message points to a feature that does not exist.
+
+Severity tally: **Critical 0 · High 3 · Medium 5 · Low 4**
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Corrupt or wrong-shaped list value crashes History, Dashboard, and Statistics with no recovery
+
+**Evidence:** `src/utils/storage.js:142-150` (`loadData` returns the parsed value as-is and only falls back to `defaultValue` on a `JSON.parse` throw or `null`); consumers that assume an array: `src/utils/storage.js:243` (`matches.findIndex`), `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoHistory.jsx:249-250` (`loadData(QM_KEY, []).sort`), `src/designs/design1-mono/landing/DashboardLanding.jsx:864-866` (`qm.sort(...).slice`), `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoStatistics.jsx:225-226` (`qm.sort`). Empirically verified: when `se_quickmatches` holds a non-array JSON value (e.g. `{}` or `5`), `loadData` returns it unchanged and `.findIndex`/`.sort`/`.filter` throw `"... is not a function"`.
+
+**What's wrong:** `loadData`'s `defaultValue` guards only parse failures, not shape. Any value that is valid JSON but not an array (schema drift across versions, a future writer that stores an object under a list key, a browser extension, or partial corruption) is returned verbatim. Every screen that calls `.sort`/`.filter`/`.findIndex` on it throws during render/mount.
+
+**User impact:** History, Dashboard, and Statistics all crash on open. Critically, the "Clear local history" recovery control lives *on* the History screen (`MonoHistory.jsx:459-472`), so the one in-app remedy is unreachable — a soft-lock. The same pattern applies to a corrupt `gs_history` via `MonoHistory.jsx:284` (`[...history].sort`). Affects all contexts (guest, signed-in, desktop, mobile, Android).
+
+**Suggested direction:** In `loadData`, coerce shape to the default's type — e.g. if `defaultValue` is an array and the parsed value is not, return the default (and optionally log/quarantine the bad value). Alternatively add `Array.isArray` guards at each call site. Trigger likelihood is low (internal writers always produce arrays); blast radius is high, hence High not Critical.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Dead `gs_*` write path feeds live "Active sessions" and "legacy history" UI that can never populate
+
+**Evidence:** `src/utils/universalStorage.js:4-9` uses a separate `gs_` keyspace (`gs_sessions`, `gs_history`, `gs_templates`, `gs_preferences`). The only writers of `gs_sessions`/`gs_history` are `src/hooks/useGameSession.js:28,84` (`saveSession`/`saveHistory`), which are called exclusively from `MonoHome.jsx`, `MonoLiveGame.jsx`, `MonoSetup.jsx` — none of which are routed in `src/designs/design1-mono/index.jsx` (verified: they reference only each other). Yet the **read** side is live: `DashboardLanding.jsx:861` (`setSessions(getActiveSessions())`) renders LIVE/PAUSED session cards at `:568-595`, and `MonoHistory.jsx:232,283-304` renders `legacyEntries` from `useGameHistory()`.
+
+**What's wrong:** The components that would create `gs_sessions`/`gs_history` data are unrouted dead code, but the dashboard active-sessions panel and history "legacy" entries still read from those stores. Additionally, the one-time prefix migration only converts `gamescore_` → `se_` (`storage.js:12-17`); it never touches `gs_*`, so any `gs_history`/`gs_sessions` left by an older build where those components were routed is orphaned but still surfaced.
+
+**User impact:** For any current-build user the dashboard "active sessions" list and history legacy entries are permanently empty dead UI; for users upgrading from an older build, stale `gs_*` data appears (and `clearMutableHistory` at `MonoHistory.jsx:395-408` does clear it, while `undoClearMutableHistory:414` writes it back to `gs_history`). Confusing and unmaintainable. Affects all contexts.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Decide one source of truth. Either route/restore the `useGameSession` write path, or remove the `getActiveSessions`/`useGameHistory` read surfaces and the `universalStorage` `gs_*` layer, and add a `gs_*` cleanup to the migration. Do not leave live readers over dead writers.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] In-progress cricket-test drafts pollute completed-match History and inflate Statistics totals
+
+**Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:405` writes a partial match into `se_quickmatches` with `status: 'in-progress'` (via `saveQuickMatch` from `storage.js`). Consumers do not filter on status: `MonoHistory.jsx:256-281` maps every `se_quickmatches` entry into a visible history row; `MonoStatistics.jsx:324` computes `totalMatches = ... + quickMatches.length` (counts every entry including in-progress); `MonoStatistics.jsx:225-226` and `DashboardLanding.jsx:864-866` load the same array unfiltered. (Tennis is clean — it writes drafts to a separate `se_tennis_quick_draft_*` key, `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:457-469`, and only writes the completed entry to `se_quickmatches` at `:489`.)
+
+**What's wrong:** A test-cricket match that the user saved mid-game (to resume later) is indistinguishable from a finished match in aggregate views. There is no `status !== 'completed'` filter on the quick-match list anywhere.
+
+**User impact:** A phantom partial-score row appears in History ("0 - 0" or a mid-innings score with no real winner), the Statistics "total matches" count is inflated by every paused test match, and the same entry can appear in the Dashboard "recent matches" top-3. The user sees games they never finished counted as played. Affects all contexts; test-cricket users specifically.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Filter `se_quickmatches` by `status === 'completed' || !!completedAt` in `MonoHistory`, `MonoStatistics`, and `DashboardLanding`, or store in-progress test drafts under a dedicated draft key like the tennis path already does.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Match IDs are `Date.now()` — collision overwrites a different match (storage and route id)
+
+**Evidence:** Quick-match result IDs: `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:936,969,1037,1142,1169,1271` all use `id: Date.now()`. Live-match route IDs also: `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:757` (`const matchId = Date.now()`, used as `id` at `:760` and as the route at `:773` `/test-match/${matchId}`) and `:778,793` (tennis `/live/${matchId}`). Dedupe/upsert keys on this id: `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:32` (`findIndex(m => m.id === match.id)`), `storage.js:243`, `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:488`. Verified: two `Date.now()` calls in the same tick return identical values.
+
+**What's wrong:** `Date.now()` is millisecond-resolution and non-unique. Two matches created in the same millisecond get the same id; the second `saveQuickMatch` then matches the first via `findIndex` and **overwrites it** rather than appending. Because the route `:matchId` is the same value, a collision also points two different live matches at the same storage record.
+
+**User impact:** A previously saved match silently disappears (replaced). Realistically rare from manual play (needs two saves within 1 ms) so latent, but it is a genuine data-loss path with zero guarding. Affects all contexts.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Use `crypto.randomUUID()` (already used elsewhere in mobile code) or `Date.now()` + a random/counter suffix for match IDs.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] No multi-tab consistency — second tab's whole-array write drops the first tab's matches
+
+**Evidence:** No `window`/`globalThis` `'storage'` event listener exists anywhere in `src` (verified by grep). Every save is read-modify-write of the entire array: `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:31-35`, `storage.js:241-250`, `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:487-489`, `saveSportTournament` `storage.js:203-214`.
+
+**What's wrong:** Two tabs (or a tab + a background-restored mobile webview) each load the full `se_quickmatches` array into memory, mutate it, and write the whole thing back. The last writer wins and clobbers any match the other tab added in the interim — it loses entire records, not just a field. No tab is notified of the other's writes, so in-memory state is also stale.
+
+**User impact:** A user scoring in two tabs, or who completes a match in tab B then saves something in tab A, loses tab B's match entirely. Web/desktop-only (Capacitor Android is single-webview). Medium because the multi-tab scenario is uncommon but the result is silent match loss.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Add a `storage`-event listener to refresh in-memory lists, and/or write individual records to per-id keys instead of one monolithic array, and/or re-read immediately before each mutate.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] "Save failed — Export your data" points to a feature that does not exist
+
+**Evidence:** The warning string appears in 10+ save paths (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:607,790`, `MonoTournamentSetup.jsx:329`, `MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:396,462`, `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:407,417,459,476`, `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:156`, etc.). Grep for any export/download/Blob/`saveAs`/`exportJSON` implementation in `src` returns nothing — no export UI or utility exists.
+
+**What's wrong:** On quota-exceeded the app instructs the user to "Export your data," but there is no export anywhere in the product. The advice is impossible to follow.
+
+**User impact:** When storage is full the user is told to take a recovery action that doesn't exist, then loses the just-played result with no recourse. Misleading and demoralizing exactly at the data-loss moment. Affects all contexts, most likely on long-lived installs (mobile/Android) where storage fills.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Either build a JSON export (download/share-sheet of all `se_*` keys) to make the message true, or change the copy to an action the user can actually take (e.g. "Delete old matches in History to free space").
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Quota-exceeded mid-match leaves a silent gap; draft and final save share the same failure with no rollback
+
+**Evidence:** `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:469-498` autosaves the scoring draft to `se_quickmatch_draft_<sport>` on every state change; `finalizeMatch` (`:642-658`) calls `persistQuickMatch` then `clearData(quickMatchDraftKey)` only `if (saved)`. `safeSave` returns `{success:false}` on quota (`storage.js:79-95`) and `saveData` returns `false` (`:133-140`).
+
+**What's wrong:** The draft autosave at `:473` has no failure handling at all — when quota is hit mid-game, the latest draft simply isn't written and no warning shows; scoring continues against an increasingly stale persisted draft. On final save the failure is surfaced (`:606-609`) and the draft is correctly *not* cleared, which is good — but there's no attempt to free space (e.g. evict the oldest completed match) so the user is stuck.
+
+**User impact:** A long match (especially test cricket with a large `history` array) can hit quota; the resume draft silently stops updating, so a crash/refresh resumes from an old point and loses recent scoring. Most likely on mobile/Android with a full store. Medium.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Surface draft-autosave failures (the effect at `:469` ignores the boolean return), and add an eviction/compaction step on quota (drop oldest completed quick match, or trim `cricketHistory` length) before declaring failure. `isStorageNearFull` (`storage.js:126`) already exists but is never consulted proactively.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] `OfflineFallback` "last quick match" reads `quick[0]`, but insertion order is engine-dependent
+
+**Evidence:** `src/components/OfflineFallback.jsx:22` returns `lastQuick: quick[0]`. Insertion order into `se_quickmatches` differs by engine: `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:34` `unshift`es new matches (newest-first → `quick[0]` is newest), but `storage.js:248` `saveQuickMatch` (used by cricket-test, `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:405,443`) `push`es to the end, and `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:489` appends with `[...withoutCurrent, entry]` (newest-last → `quick[0]` is oldest).
+
+**What's wrong:** `OfflineFallback` is the only consumer that relies on raw array order (every other view re-sorts by date: `MonoHistory.jsx:250,308`, `DashboardLanding.jsx:865`, `MonoStatistics.jsx:226`). For a user whose most recent match was test-cricket or tennis, `quick[0]` is their *oldest* match, so the offline "last match" card shows the wrong game.
+
+**User impact:** Offline screen displays a stale/incorrect "last match." Minor and offline-only, but a visible wrong result. Affects all contexts when offline.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Sort by `completedAt || date || createdAt` in `readOfflineSnapshot` instead of trusting `quick[0]`; longer-term, standardize all three save paths on one insertion convention.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Prefix migration is one-shot and gated by `se_migrated`, with no re-run on partial failure
+
+**Evidence:** `storage.js:5-27` — `migrateStoragePrefix` returns early if `se_migrated` is set (`:8`), copies `gamescore_*` to `se_*` only when the target is empty (`:18`), then sets `se_migrated=1` unconditionally (`:22`) even though individual `setItem` calls could throw on quota.
+
+**What's wrong:** If a `setItem` during migration throws (quota), the outer `try` swallows it and... actually the throw skips the `se_migrated` write, so it would retry next load — acceptable. But once `se_migrated` is set, newly-appearing `gamescore_*` keys (e.g. from a synced/imported profile) are never migrated. Low risk given `gamescore_` is legacy.
+
+**User impact:** Edge-case stranded legacy data. Negligible for current users.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Make the prefix migration idempotent (run the scan every load; it's cheap) rather than gating on a one-time flag, or drop it entirely once the legacy window has passed.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] `loadQuickMatch` coerces `Number(matchId)` — IDs near `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`/non-numeric mismatch
+
+**Evidence:** `storage.js:256-259` `find(m => m.id === matchId || m.id === Number(matchId))`; route ids come from `Date.now()` (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:757,778`).
+
+**What's wrong:** The dual string/number compare papers over the fact that ids are stored sometimes as numbers (`id: Date.now()`) and matched against string route params. It works today because `Date.now()` is a safe-integer, but it's a fragile coupling — any future non-numeric id (e.g. a UUID, which the id-collision fix would introduce) would still match by string only, which is fine, but mixed types in the same array would be inconsistent.
+
+**User impact:** None currently; a latent footgun for the recommended UUID migration.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Standardize id type (string) end-to-end so equality is unambiguous.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] `getStorageUsage` can't measure the memory fallback, so quota warnings are silently disabled in Safari private mode
+
+**Evidence:** `storage.js:101-121` only iterates `globalThis.localStorage` when `storageAvailable` is true; for the memory fallback it returns `used: 0`. `isStorageNearFull` (`:126-129`) therefore always reports `false` in private mode.
+
+**What's wrong:** In Safari private mode (memory fallback active), there is no usage accounting, so any proactive "near full" UX would never fire. The memory store is also wiped on tab close, which the user is never warned about.
+
+**User impact:** Safari-private-mode users get no near-full warning and lose all data on tab close with no notice. Niche but a real silent-data-loss surface.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Track an approximate byte count in the memory fallback, and surface a one-time "private mode — data won't persist" notice when `isPrivateMode` is true.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] `loadPreference` JSON-parses plain strings, risking type drift for string preferences
+
+**Evidence:** `storage.js:165-190` — `savePreference` stores a plain string as-is (`:167`), but `loadPreference` tries `JSON.parse` first (`:182`) and only falls back to the raw string on throw. A stored preference string that happens to be valid JSON (e.g. `"true"`, `"123"`, `"null"`) round-trips to a different type (`boolean`, `number`, `null`) than what was saved.
+
+**What's wrong:** Asymmetric serialize/deserialize. A preference saved as the string `"123"` loads back as the number `123`; saved as `"null"` loads as `null`.
+
+**User impact:** A preference whose value is a numeric/boolean-looking string is read back wrong. Low likelihood (most prefs are identifiers/enums) but a correctness trap.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Store a typed wrapper (`{t:'string',v:...}`) or always `JSON.stringify` on save and `JSON.parse` on load symmetrically.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
