+# Shared state, hooks & error handling
+
+## Summary
+
+Audited the shared hooks (`useGameSession`, `useMatchSync`, `useTimer`, `useDebounce`), the error surfaces (`ErrorBoundary`, `OfflineFallback`), and the Sentry/provider bootstrap in `main.jsx`, tracing each into its live consumers. The hooks themselves are mostly clean (good `isMounted` guards, correct `clearInterval`/`clearTimeout` cleanup, functional-updater scoring). The real risks are in error-boundary *coverage*: the app's root providers in `main.jsx` and several siblings in `index.jsx` render **outside** any boundary, so a throw there (notably with Convex/Clerk env missing or a nav-chrome bug) is an uncaught white screen — the worst case for the task's "env MISSING" context. Secondary issues: the live match timer drifts/undercounts because it relies on `setInterval` with no wall-clock anchor (and timed tournament matches lose the clock entirely on draft-resume), the `ErrorBoundary` never resets on in-app navigation, and `useGameSession` is dead code carrying a side-effect-in-reducer smell.
+
+Severity tally: **critical 0 · high 2 · medium 3 · low 4**
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Root providers in main.jsx render outside any error boundary — env-missing / provider init throw = white screen
+
+**Evidence:** `src/main.jsx:262-313`. `RootApp` returns `CloudAuthRoot` / `ConvexProvider` / `LocalAuthProvider` trees and is rendered by `ReactDOM.createRoot(...).render(<React.StrictMode><RootApp /></React.StrictMode>)` with **no `ErrorBoundary` anywhere above the providers**. The only `ErrorBoundary` instances live deeper, inside `src/designs/design1-mono/index.jsx:1202` (route tree) and around `MonoProfile` (1215-1216). `App.jsx` (the lazy `Design` parent) also has no boundary — only a `Suspense`.
+
+**What's wrong:** Anything that throws during provider construction or first render — `ConvexReactClient` setup, the Clerk provider inside `CloudAuthRoot` when `VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`/`VITE_CONVEX_URL` is missing or malformed, or `getAuthBootstrapMode` — escapes upward to the React root and unmounts the whole tree. There is no boundary to catch it, so the user sees a blank page with no recovery UI and (because Sentry is initialized lazily via `initSentryAfterStartup`, `main.jsx:127-151`) possibly no captured exception either.
+
+**User impact:** Signed-in user, or any user, on a build with a broken/absent Convex/Clerk env (exactly the "env MISSING" context called out in scope) gets a hard white screen with no "Try again / Home" affordance. Mobile-web and Capacitor are most exposed because their config is supplied at build/runtime and is easy to get wrong.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Wrap the entire `RootApp` render (or at minimum the provider subtree) in an `ErrorBoundary` at `main.jsx`, with copy tuned for a fatal boot failure. Initialize Sentry eagerly enough (or capture in the boundary's `componentDidCatch`) that boot crashes are still reported.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Live match timer drifts and is suspended in background/locked screens — timed matches end at the wrong wall-clock time
+
+**Evidence:** `src/hooks/useTimer.js:40-55` ticks with `setInterval(() => setElapsed(prev => prev + 1), 1000)` and has **no wall-clock anchor** — `elapsed` is purely a count of fired ticks. Consumed by `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:53` for timed-mode auto-finish: `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:137` ends the match when `timer.elapsed >= timeLimit`, and `:389` shows the on-screen countdown `timeLimit - timer.elapsed`. Notably the *resume* path already does this correctly — `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:215-221` (`getRestoredTimerElapsed`) reconstructs elapsed from `Date.now() - savedAt` — proving the wall-clock pattern is known but is not applied to the live tick.
+
+**What's wrong:** Browsers throttle and fully suspend `setInterval` in backgrounded tabs and when the device screen locks (mobile-web and Capacitor WebView). While suspended, no ticks fire, so `timer.elapsed` undercounts true elapsed time. A 10-minute timed half can run several minutes long in real time, and the displayed countdown freezes while backgrounded. There is no `visibilitychange`/`Date.now()` reconciliation.
+
+**User impact:** Referee/scorer on a timed football/basketball/etc. match (signed-in or guest, any platform; worst on Android/iOS where the screen locks) sees the clock pause and the match auto-end late. The match result a user "would notice" is wrong relative to real game time.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Anchor the timer to wall-clock — store a `startedAt` epoch and compute `elapsed = floor((Date.now() - startedAt)/1000) + accumulatedPauseAdjustment` on each tick and on `visibilitychange`, instead of `prev + 1`. Reuse the `getRestoredTimerElapsed` approach for the live tick.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Nav chrome (GlobalNavigation, OfflineFallback, OnboardingReminder) renders outside the route ErrorBoundary
+
+**Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/index.jsx:1197-1202`. `GlobalNavigation` (1197), `OfflineFallback` (1198) and `OnboardingReminder` (1199) are siblings rendered **before** `<ErrorBoundary ...>` opens at 1202. The boundary only wraps `OnboardingGuard`/`Routes` (1203-1248).
+
+**What's wrong:** A render-time throw in the persistent nav header, the offline banner, or the onboarding reminder is not caught by the route boundary and propagates to the next boundary up — which (per the high finding above) does not exist until the React root. Result: full white screen instead of a contained route-level error.
+
+**User impact:** Any user; a bug in always-mounted chrome takes down the whole app rather than degrading one region. `OfflineFallback` reads `globalThis.navigator.onLine` and localStorage on every online/offline event (`OfflineFallback.jsx:13-29,41-55`), so a storage/parse error there would blank the app.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Wrap each persistent-chrome sibling in its own lightweight `ErrorBoundary` (with `captureToSentry`) so a chrome failure degrades gracefully and leaves the routed content usable.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Timed tournament matches lose the entire clock on draft-save/resume
+
+**Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx`. `saveDraft` (262-274) persists `draftState: { score1, score2, history, savedAt }` — **no timer value**. The restore path (110-114) sets `score1/score2/history` from `draftState` but never calls `timer.restore(...)` and never re-sets `timerStarted`. Contrast `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:463` which does `timer.restore(getRestoredTimerElapsed(draft), false)`.
+
+**What's wrong:** For a timed tournament match, saving a draft and resuming (or any reload mid-match) resets `timer.elapsed` to 0 while keeping the score. Because `timerStarted` also isn't restored, the start effect (`MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:123-129`) won't even re-arm the clock until the next score. The match clock — central to timed mode — is silently lost.
+
+**User impact:** Signed-in or guest user running a timed tournament match who navigates away/reloads loses elapsed time; the auto-finish at `timeLimit` is now measured from a wrong zero, so the match runs far too long. Any platform.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Persist `timerElapsed` (and `timerStarted`) into `draftState` and restore via `timer.restore(...)` on load, mirroring the MonoQuickMatch resume logic.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] ErrorBoundary does not reset on in-app navigation (no resetKeys / location-keyed reset)
+
+**Evidence:** `src/components/ErrorBoundary.jsx:5-46`. State `hasError` is only cleared by the explicit `handleRetry` button (`:33-35`) or a full reload/assign (`:29-31,37-43`). There is no `componentDidUpdate`/`resetKeys` tied to the router location.
+
+**What's wrong:** Once a route throws and the boundary shows its fallback, navigating with the SPA router (`navigate()` / `<Link>`) — e.g. via a `GlobalNavigation` link that is itself outside the boundary, or browser back — does not reset `hasError`. The boundary keeps rendering the error screen over whatever route is now active. The provided escape buttons use `globalThis.location.assign('/')` / `reload()` (full document loads), which do work, but normal in-app links appear dead until a hard reload.
+
+**User impact:** After any caught route crash, in-app navigation looks broken; only Reload/Home/Play (hard loads) recover. Medium because hard-load escapes exist.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Add a `resetKeys` prop (or read `useLocation` in a wrapper) and clear `hasError` when the location changes, so SPA navigation auto-recovers the boundary.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] useMatchSync deferred "bootstrap" sync is effectively unreachable and, if it fired, would leave persisted sync metadata stale
+
+**Evidence:** `src/hooks/useMatchSync.js:71-124`. The pending-bootstrap branch in `syncMatch` (112-121) only arms when `isAuthenticated && !user`; the effect at 81-89 then calls `executeSync` directly, which updates internal `syncState` but cannot call back into the consumer's `applySyncStateToResult`. However, of the two callers in `MonoQuickMatch.jsx`, `syncCompletedMatch` (630) is guarded by `if (!isAuthenticated || !user) { resetSync(); return; }` so it never arms the pending path, and `retryResultSync` (706) requires a non-null `result` plus a visible "failed" retry button. `result` is never restored from storage (`setResult` only at `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:620,653,732,1304`; null on fresh load), so the `isAuthenticated && !user` window is not practically hit. Even if it were, nothing renders the persisted `sync.status` (no `sync.status` read in `MonoHistory`), so a stale `'syncing'` value is never shown.
+
+**What's wrong:** A latent design gap: the deferred-sync success/failure never reconciles back to the persisted record. It is currently masked by the guards above.
+
+**User impact:** None observable today; defensive/latent. Worth a guard or a callback so it cannot silently desync if a future caller removes the `!user` guard.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Have the bootstrap effect surface its result via a callback/ref the consumer can subscribe to, or drop the unused pending path. Low priority.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] useGameSession is dead code and calls saveHistory as a side effect inside a setState updater
+
+**Evidence:** `src/hooks/useGameSession.js`. Only consumers are `MonoLiveGame.jsx:18` and `MonoSetup.jsx:9`, neither of which is imported by the router (`grep MonoSetup|MonoLiveGame` in `src/designs/design1-mono/index.jsx` → no matches; both symbols appear only in their own definitions). Inside `completeGame` (74-88), `createHistoryRecord` + `saveHistory(record)` and the `onComplete` callback run **inside** the `setSession(prev => {...})` updater (83-85) — a side effect in a reducer, which React StrictMode intentionally double-invokes in dev.
+
+**What's wrong:** (a) The hook ships but is unreachable, so it's untested surface that can rot. (b) The side-effect-in-updater means under StrictMode dev (and any future Concurrent re-invocation) `saveHistory`/`onComplete` can fire twice, double-writing history.
+
+**User impact:** None in production today (unrouted). Would become a duplicate-history bug if these screens are ever wired up.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Either delete `useGameSession`/`MonoLiveGame`/`MonoSetup` or, if kept, move `saveHistory`/`onComplete` out of the updater into the caller after state commits.
+
+**Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Auto-save effect persists on initial load (write amplification, and can re-save a just-deleted session)
+
+**Evidence:** `src/hooks/useGameSession.js:24-29`. The auto-save effect runs `saveSession(session)` on every `session` change with no "dirty since load" guard, so loading an existing session (16-22) immediately rewrites it. `removeGame` (109-114) deletes then `setSession(null)`, which is fine, but any code path that sets a fresh-loaded session triggers a redundant write.
+
+**What's wrong:** Minor write amplification and a subtle ordering smell. Dead code today, so impact is theoretical.
+
+**User impact:** None currently (hook unrouted). If revived, extra localStorage writes on every load.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Track a `dirtyRef`/skip-first-render guard so auto-save only fires after a real mutation.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] OfflineFallback assumes navigator exists at module/init time
+
+**Evidence:** `src/components/OfflineFallback.jsx:29` initializes state with `globalThis.navigator.onLine` directly (no `typeof navigator` guard), and `readOfflineSnapshot` (13-25) reads localStorage during render initialization.
+
+**What's wrong:** In any non-DOM render context (SSR/test harness without a `navigator`, or a hardened WebView) this throws during the initial state initializer. Other call sites in the codebase guard with `typeof navigator === 'undefined'` (e.g. `main.jsx:33`), so this is an inconsistency rather than a confirmed crash in the shipped browser/Capacitor runtime.
+
+**User impact:** None on standard web/Android where `navigator` exists; defensive nit. Because this component renders outside the route boundary (see medium finding), a throw here would white-screen the app.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Guard with `typeof navigator !== 'undefined' ? navigator.onLine : true`, consistent with `main.jsx`.
+
+**Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+## Notes on things checked and found OK
+
+- `useTimer` cleanup is correct: unmount clears the interval (`useTimer.js:9-14`) and the `isRunning` effect clears+nulls on every toggle and on cleanup (`:47-54`); the `isMounted` guard prevents setState after unmount.
+- `useDebounce` is correct: the effect clears the prior timeout on `value`/`delay` change and on unmount (`useDebounce.js:5-8`); no stale-closure issue.
+- `useMatchSync` uses refs (`payloadRef`, `syncInFlightRef`) to avoid stale closures across the async mutation, and `executeSync`'s `finally` always clears the in-flight flag (`useMatchSync.js:43-69`).
+- `ErrorBoundary` correctly gates the dev-only stack via `import.meta.env.DEV` and guards `captureToSentry !== false` (`ErrorBoundary.jsx:16,60`).
