+# Cricket — Limited-Overs Engine Audit
+
+Scope: `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx` (the live scorer used for any non-Test cricket tournament match, routed via `MonoTournamentLiveScore.jsx:27-29` when `format.totalInnings !== 4`) and `src/utils/cricketCalculations.js` (pure helpers: run rate, NRR, winner/result, points table). Cross-referenced against `src/utils/cricketCalculations.test.js` and `src/utils/cricketRunControls.test.js`.
+
+## Summary
+
+The pure-math helpers (run rate, NRR, balls↔overs, points table) are well-tested and correct. The live scorer, however, is **not unit-tested at all** — every finding below lives in component logic that the green suite never exercises. The most serious problems are scoring-correctness gaps a real scorer would hit immediately: a no-ball/wide that does not let you add runs scored off the bat (the extra is hard-coded to +1), a free-hit that can be lost to a normal "Wicket" press because the wicket handler ignores the free-hit flag, no strike rotation / required-run-rate at all, a winner computed purely on total runs (ignoring the explicit super-over / wickets-in-hand result for the points table), and a `saveMatch()` invoked **inside the render body** during the super-over result. There is also a stale-state innings-switch path and an all-out NRR mis-attribution edge.
+
+Severity tally: **Critical 3 · High 5 · Medium 5 · Low 4** (17 findings).
+
+---
+
+### [CRITICAL] No-ball / wide always adds exactly +1 — runs scored off the delivery are unrecordable
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:279-300` (`addExtra`). The handler always does `runs: prev[key].runs + 1` and never increments `balls` (correct: extras don't consume a legal ball). But there is **no way to record runs taken off a no-ball or wide** (e.g. a no-ball hit for four = 5 runs to the batting side; byes run off a wide). The two extra buttons are literally labelled `Wide (+1)` / `No Ball (+1)` (`:761-768`) and both call `addExtra` with a fixed +1.
+
+What's wrong: every boundary or running pair off a no-ball/wide is under-counted by up to 6 runs. On a free-hit (which only arises from a no-ball, `:297-299`), the batting side can score 4 or 6 — the single most valuable ball in the format — and the scorer can only add +1, then must press a numbered run button which *consumes a legal ball that should not have been bowled*.
+
+User impact: wrong team totals in any match with extras hit for runs; chases and the points-table NRR derived from those totals are wrong. A user watching a real over will see the on-screen score diverge from reality.
+
+Suggested direction: after an extra, allow a follow-up run entry (extra +1 plus runs off the bat) without consuming a legal ball; or provide "No-ball + N" inputs.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [CRITICAL] Free-hit is silently lost on a normal "Wicket" press — `addWicket` ignores `freeHit`
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:242-276` (`addWicket`) unconditionally does `team.wickets += 1` and `if (freeHit) setFreeHit(false)` (`:254`). On a free hit the batter can only be dismissed run-out, yet the same full-width button at `:771-775` shows the label `Run Out Only` while still calling the *same* `addWicket`, which credits a full wicket and (if it is the last wicket) sets `allOut = true` / ends the innings.
+
+What's wrong: pressing the button on a free hit records an illegal dismissal. There is no branch that treats a free-hit "wicket" as run-out-only versus a normal wicket — the label changes but the behaviour does not.
+
+User impact: a side can be wrongly given a wicket (or declared all-out / innings-ended) on a free hit where only a run-out is legal. Directly corrupts wickets, all-out detection, and the win-by-wickets margin.
+
+Suggested direction: on `freeHit`, only allow a run-out wicket and/or guard the wicket handler so a non-run-out press is a no-op while keeping the free hit live.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [CRITICAL] Points-table winner ignores wickets-in-hand and the super-over result — uses raw run totals only
+
+`cricketCalculations.js:170-187` (`getMatchWinner`) for limited-overs returns `tie` whenever `team1Score.runs === team2Score.runs`, and otherwise the higher run total. But a chase is won **the moment the target is passed with wickets in hand**, and a real tie can be broken by a super over. The live scorer *does* persist `match.winner` (`saveMatch` `:453`) and a `superOver` object (`:454`), and `getMatchWinner` honours `match.winner` first (`:172`) — **but only if it was saved**. `calculateCricketPointsTable` (`:309-352`) calls `getMatchWinner(match)` and, for a match completed via the run-comparison path, a level score is scored as a tie (1 pt each) even though a super over was played and decided.
+
+Concretely: a match tied off the bat then won on a super over is stored with `winner = soWinner` by `saveMatch(soWinner)` (`:628`), so `getMatchWinner` returns it — OK. **However** `getLimitedOversResult` (`:242-256`) and any display that recomputes the result from `team1Score`/`team2Score` will still print "Match Tied" because it never consults `match.winner` or `match.superOver`. The result string and the standings can disagree.
+
+User impact: a super-over win is shown as a tie in result text / history; standings and result-line contradict each other.
+
+Suggested direction: make `getLimitedOversResult` consult `match.winner`/`match.superOver` first, mirroring `getMatchWinner`.
+
+Confidence: high (the divergence between `getMatchWinner` honouring `match.winner` and `getLimitedOversResult` not doing so is in the source).
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] `saveMatch()` is called inside the render body during the super-over result
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:592-630`. When `superOverPhase === 'result'` and a winner exists, the component executes `saveMatch(soWinner); return null;` (`:627-629`) **directly in the render path**, not in an effect or handler. `saveMatch` calls `setSaveWarning`, `saveSportTournament`, `saveTournamentToConvex`, and schedules `navigate` — i.e. side effects and state updates during render.
+
+What's wrong: state-update-in-render is a React anti-pattern; under StrictMode/double-render or any re-render before navigation it can fire `saveMatch` more than once (duplicate Convex writes, duplicate confetti/haptics at `:434-437`) and can throw "Cannot update during render" warnings.
+
+User impact: possible double-save of the completed match / duplicate sync payloads; flaky completion.
+
+Suggested direction: move the auto-save into a `useEffect` keyed on `superOverPhase === 'result' && soWinner`.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [HIGH] No strike rotation and no per-batsman tracking
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx` (whole file) has no concept of a striker/non-striker. A grep for `strike|striker|rotat|swap` returns nothing. Odd runs (1/3/5) and over completion should swap the strike; the app never models who is on strike.
+
+What's wrong: for an app that surfaces powerplay, free-hit and over counters, the absence of strike means it cannot ever show batter scores or "keep strike" context, and any future per-ball commentary is impossible.
+
+User impact: scorers expecting batsman-level detail get only a team total; functionally a "team run counter," not a cricket scorecard.
+
+Suggested direction: out of scope for a hot-fix, but document the limitation; if batter stats are a goal, add striker state that swaps on odd runs and over end.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [HIGH] No required run rate (only current run rate) shown during a chase
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:693-695` shows `RR` (current run rate) always, and the target line (`:717-723`) shows "Need X from Y balls" — but never the **required run rate**, the single most-watched chase number. `cricketCalculations.js` exposes `calculateRunRate` but no RRR helper.
+
+User impact: during a run chase the user cannot see the asking rate; reduces the scorer to mental arithmetic on a screen that otherwise advertises run-rate awareness.
+
+Suggested direction: when `target !== null` and balls remain, show `RRR = (runsNeeded * 6) / ballsRemaining`.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [HIGH] Stale `battingTeam` closure in the `pendingInningsSwitch` effect
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:138-145`. The effect that performs the innings change runs with dependency array `[pendingInningsSwitch]` only, yet reads `battingTeam` (`setBattingTeam(battingTeam === 1 ? 2 : 1)`). Because the effect closure is recreated every render, it normally captures a fresh `battingTeam` — but the linter-suppressed single-dep array is fragile: if any future change batches the `setPendingInningsSwitch(true)` together with a `battingTeam` update, the effect can read a stale value and **fail to flip the batting side**, leaving the wrong team "batting" in innings 2.
+
+What's wrong: the innings-switch correctness depends on render timing rather than on an explicit, self-contained transition. The flip also assumes the team that batted first is always team-1-by-index; it derives the new batting team from the *current* `battingTeam`, which is fine today but couples two pieces of state through an effect.
+
+User impact: a mis-fire would put the already-batted side back in to bat in the second innings (or never switch), corrupting the entire second innings.
+
+Suggested direction: compute the next batting team inside `addRuns`/`addWicket` at the moment the innings ends and set both `innings` and `battingTeam` together, or include `battingTeam` in the dep array and guard against double-runs.
+
+Confidence: medium (the bug is latent/timing-dependent, not a guaranteed mis-fire today).
+
+### [HIGH] All-out NRR uses full quota of overs even when the side was bowled out early — but only when `format.overs` is set
+
+`cricketCalculations.js:281-286` (`accumulateLimitedOversStats`) sets `ballsFaced = teamScore.allOut ? totalBalls : teamScore.balls`, and `totalBalls` (`:334`) is `format.overs * 6` **or, when `format.overs` is absent, `team1Score.balls`**. For a custom/gully format with no `overs` (overs `null`), an all-out side gets `ballsFaced = team1Score.balls` (team 1's faced balls) regardless of which team it is — team 2's all-out innings is credited team 1's ball count.
+
+What's wrong: NRR for unlimited/over-less custom formats is computed against the wrong ball denominator; the tested case only covers `format: { overs: 10 }` (`cricketCalculations.test.js:275-291`), so this path is uncovered.
+
+User impact: wrong NRR → wrong standings order for custom-format tournaments where a side is bowled out.
+
+Suggested direction: when `format.overs` is null, fall back to each side's own `balls` (or document that NRR is undefined for unlimited formats).
+
+Confidence: medium.
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] `isMatchComplete` lets a chase that exactly equals the target be treated as a tie, but the "End Match" button can be pressed at any time and saves a possibly-wrong winner
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:431-447` (`saveMatch` default path) decides the winner purely by `scores.team1.runs > scores.team2.runs`. The "End Match" button (`:792-799`) is always enabled (only gated by `scoringPrompt.isInteractionLocked`), even mid-first-innings. Pressing it before the chase is complete records whoever currently has more runs as the winner — e.g. ending during innings 1 makes the batting side "win" by runs with the bowling side on 0.
+
+User impact: an accidental early "End Match" stores a nonsensical completed result (and fires confetti); recoverable only by re-scoring.
+
+Suggested direction: disable/confirm "End Match" until `isMatchComplete`, or compute the winner with target/wickets semantics rather than raw run totals.
+
+Confidence: high (button is unconditionally enabled; winner is raw-run-based).
+
+### [MEDIUM] Win-by-wickets margin uses `format.players - 1`, ignoring `lastManStands`
+
+`cricketCalculations.js:252-255` (`getLimitedOversResult`) computes `maxWickets = (format.players || 11) - 1`, but the live engine's own `getMaxWickets` (`:138-142`) returns `players` (not `players-1`) when `format.lastManStands` is set. So for a Last-Man-Stands match the result string reports one fewer wicket in hand than the engine actually allowed.
+
+User impact: "Won by N wickets" off by one for Last-Man-Stands / gully formats.
+
+Suggested direction: have `getLimitedOversResult` call `getMaxWickets(match.format)` instead of inlining `players - 1`.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [MEDIUM] Super over: 1-run wins are mis-described and a tie-on-wickets is not broken
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:592-595` decides the super-over winner on `superOver.team1.runs` vs `team2.runs` only. Real super-over rules break a tied super over by boundaries hit; here equal runs always loops to "another super over" (`:597-625`). More notably, the chase target text at `:556` reads `Target: {soTarget + 1} · Need {max(0, soTarget+1 - runs)} from {6 - balls} balls` and the second team keeps batting until `balls >= 6 || wickets >= 2` (`:531`) — it does **not** stop the instant the target is passed, so a side that has already won can be made to face more balls (and concede a wicket that flips nothing but looks wrong).
+
+User impact: super over doesn't end on the winning run; cosmetic but confusing, and the "another super over" loop can be hit when the laws would have decided it.
+
+Suggested direction: end team-2's super over as soon as `runs > team1.runs`; optionally break ties on boundaries.
+
+Confidence: medium.
+
+### [MEDIUM] `currentOver` for powerplay counts only legal balls but resets the phase using `balls/6+1`, so the powerplay banner flips one ball early/late around extras
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:178-181`: `currentOver = floor(balls/6) + 1`. Because extras never increment `balls` (correct), this is fine for legal balls — but `getPowerplayPhase` (`cricketCalculations.js:122-131`) is inclusive on both ends (`over >= start && over <= end`). After the final legal ball of the last powerplay over (e.g. ball 6 of over 6 in T20, `balls` becomes 6 → `currentOver` = 2... wait, at `balls=36` → over 7), the banner disappears exactly at the over boundary, which is correct; but mid-over the label shows the *upcoming* over's status. Edge: at `balls=0` it shows over 1 (correct). No functional score impact.
+
+User impact: minor — powerplay label may look off by a fraction around boundaries; no scoring effect.
+
+Suggested direction: confirm intended display; consider labelling "in over N" explicitly.
+
+Confidence: medium (display-only).
+
+### [MEDIUM] `target` line keeps showing "Need N from M balls" after the match is already won
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:717-723`. The target block renders whenever `target !== null` (innings 2), independent of `isMatchComplete`. Once the chase succeeds (`runs > target`), `isMatchComplete` is true and the scoring controls hide (`:741`), but the target line can still read "Need 0 from K balls" alongside a completed badge.
+
+User impact: minor contradictory text on the completion screen.
+
+Suggested direction: hide the target line when `isMatchComplete`.
+
+Confidence: high (cheap to confirm: the block has no `isMatchComplete` guard).
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Hard-coded legacy blue `#0066ff` throughout the scorer violates the green-monotone design system
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:555, 673, 677, 699, 719, 782` use `#0066ff` for target/powerplay/trial-ball/free-hit text. Per project design memory the old blue is dead and accents must come from `var(--primary)` (field green).
+
+User impact: off-brand colour on the cricket scorer only.
+
+Suggested direction: replace literals with CSS variables.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [LOW] Numbered run buttons include `5`, but extras/byes are absent — inconsistent run vocabulary
+
+`cricketRunControls.js:1` exposes `[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]` and the keyboard handler (`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:415`) accepts "5". Five off the bat is rare but legal; meanwhile common run sources (byes, leg-byes, 2-off-a-no-ball) have no control. The vocabulary is lopsided.
+
+User impact: minor — UI offers an uncommon value while omitting common ones.
+
+Suggested direction: reconsider control set alongside the extras fix.
+
+Confidence: medium.
+
+### [LOW] `saveSnapshot` caps history at 100 but draft persists only 50 — undo depth silently differs after a pause/resume
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:184-192` keeps the last 100 snapshots in memory; `saveDraft` (`:387`) persists only `history.slice(-50)`. After Pause Match + resume, undo depth halves with no indication.
+
+User impact: minor — fewer undos available after resuming a paused match than before.
+
+Suggested direction: align the two limits or document.
+
+Confidence: high.
+
+### [LOW] `isInningsComplete` disables run/extra/wicket buttons, but extras (which don't consume a ball) become un-addable at the over/innings boundary
+
+`MonoCricketLiveScore.jsx:761-773`: the Wide/No-Ball buttons are disabled when `isInningsComplete`. Since `isInningsComplete` triggers on `balls >= totalBalls`, the very delivery that *should* be re-bowled because it was a wide/no-ball on the last legal ball cannot be entered — the only legal continuation of the innings (a wide that keeps the innings alive) is blocked.
+
+User impact: rare edge — cannot record a wide/no-ball bowled when the legal-ball quota is exactly met; innings ends a delivery early.
+
+Suggested direction: keep extras enabled while `wickets < maxWickets`, since extras don't consume the ball quota.
+
+Confidence: medium.
