+# Cricket Test-Match Engine Audit
+
+Scope: `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx` and the calc utilities it consumes (`src/utils/cricketCalculations.js`). Focus: multi-innings (up to 4), declaration, follow-on enforcement/eligibility, lead/deficit display, draw vs result, innings transitions, reload persistence, undo, and end-of-innings/match boundary correctness.
+
+## Summary
+
+The pure calculation helpers (`getTestMatchResult`, `buildTestWinResult`, `canEnforceFollowOn`) are sound and partially covered by the green suite via `getMatchWinner`. The defects live in the **component's gating logic**, which has **zero direct tests** — and they are serious. A wrong `checkResult` guard ends the match on the **first scoring stroke of the 3rd innings** whenever the side batting third already leads on aggregate (the common case), making the advertised 4-innings flow unplayable and unrecoverable. A second end-condition gap mislabels follow-on innings victories as "Won by N wickets," and follow-on is structurally offered only to team 1. There is also no auto-persistence, so an unpaused long match is wiped on reload.
+
+Severity tally: **critical 1 · high 3 · medium 2 · low 3**
+
+---
+
+### [CRITICAL] 3rd innings ends on the first scoring event whenever the third-batting side already leads
+
+**Evidence:** `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:192-207` (`checkResult` guards only `currentInningsIndex < 2`), called from `addRuns` (lines 274-276), `addWicket` (line 311), and `addExtra` (line 334) whenever `currentInningsIndex >= 2`.
+
+**What's wrong:** `checkResult` declares the match over as soon as `battingTotal > bowlingTotal` for any innings at index >= 2. In the 3rd innings (index 2) the batting side is, in the overwhelmingly common case, the side that led after the first two innings (team 1 led, declined/was ineligible for follow-on, and bats again). Trace: Inn0 team1 = 300, Inn1 team2 = 250, advance to index 2 (team1 batting). The very first run makes `battingTotal = 301 > bowlingTotal = 250` → `setMatchComplete(true)`, result "Won by 51 runs." The third innings cannot be played at all. Real test cricket requires the third innings to extend the lead and the fourth innings to be bowled out / chased. The guard should be `currentInningsIndex < 3` (only the 4th innings can end the match by overtaking the target).
+
+**User impact:** Core 4-innings test flow is broken. The match ends the instant the leading side scores in its second innings, producing a nonsense margin and skipping innings 3 (extension) and 4 (the chase) entirely. There is **no Undo on the match-complete screen** (undo lives only in the active scoring UI, lines 383-390 / 774-780), so the user cannot recover — they can only "Save & Return" a wrong result or discard the whole match.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Change the `checkResult` early-return to `if (currentInningsIndex < 3) return false;` so only the 4th innings can trigger a mid-innings finish, and route 3rd-innings completion through `advanceInnings` (which already handles all-out/declared/over-limit transitions).
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Follow-on innings victory is mislabeled "Won by N wickets" (missing innings-victory short-circuit after the 3rd innings)
+
+**Evidence:** `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:210-232` (`advanceInnings`); innings-victory logic lives only in `buildTestWinResult` (`cricketCalculations.js:195-208`), which is reached via `getTestMatchResult` only when `nextIndex >= 4` (line 223) or via `checkResult`.
+
+**What's wrong:** When team 2 follows on and is dismissed in the 3rd innings still trailing on aggregate, the match should end "Won by an innings and N runs" (team 1 batted once, team 2 twice — exactly the `winPlayed === 1 && losePlayed === 2` case in `buildTestWinResult`). But `advanceInnings` at the end of innings 3 computes `nextIndex = 3`, which is `< 4`, so it does **not** call `getTestMatchResult`; it simply advances to a 4th innings (`setCurrentInningsIndex(3)`). The 4th innings has team 1 batting (per the follow-on swap at lines 242-243), and `checkResult` fires on the first stroke because team 1 already leads — producing "Won by N wickets" via the `lastInnings.teamId === winnerId` branch (`cricketCalculations.js:202-207`) instead of "Won by an innings and N runs." The result is factually wrong and an extra phantom innings is started.
+
+**User impact:** A genuine innings victory after an enforced follow-on is reported with the wrong result type and margin; a meaningless 4th innings is also spun up. Users who follow the game will notice immediately.
+
+**Suggested direction:** In `advanceInnings`, after computing `updatedInnings`, evaluate `getTestMatchResult` whenever the trailing side has completed its 2nd innings (all-out/declared) and still trails — i.e. detect the innings-victory / completed-match condition before blindly advancing to index 3. Reuse `countPlayedInnings` semantics so the existing `buildTestWinResult` branch is hit.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Follow-on can only ever be offered to team 1; if team 2 leads by 200+, no follow-on option exists
+
+**Evidence:** `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:214-220` calls `canEnforceFollowOn(updatedInnings[0].runs, updatedInnings[1].runs)` — always `t1 - t2`; the prompt UI hardcodes team 1 as the leader (`lead = innings[0].runs - innings[1].runs`, line 548; "{team1Name} leads by {lead} runs" / "Force {team2Name} to bat again", lines 559-562); `canEnforceFollowOn` itself only checks `innings1Runs - innings2Runs >= 200` (`cricketCalculations.js:147-149`).
+
+**What's wrong:** The follow-on margin is computed solely from team1−team2. If team 2 (batting second) builds the 200+ lead, `canEnforceFollowOn(t1, t2)` returns false (the difference is negative), so the prompt never appears and team 2 cannot enforce the follow-on on team 1. The prompt copy is also unconditionally framed around team 1 leading, so even the swap logic (lines 240-248, which is itself correct for the team-1-leads case) is never exercised for the symmetric case. Note `lead` at line 548 can also render **negative** if the prompt were ever shown with team 2 ahead.
+
+**User impact:** Half of all legitimate follow-on scenarios (team batting second takes the big lead) are silently unsupported. The leading side is denied a rule-legal decision, and any displayed lead text would be wrong/negative.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Compute the lead as `abs` and determine the leader dynamically; pass the actual leader/trailer into `canEnforceFollowOn`; drive the prompt copy and the innings-3/4 team swap from the computed leader rather than hardcoded team 1.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] No auto-persistence — an unpaused long match is wiped on reload
+
+**Evidence:** Load effect `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:76-137` restores progress only from `qm.draftState` / `foundMatch.draftState`; otherwise it resets to fresh `initInnings`. The only writer of `draftState` is `saveDraft` (lines 393-425), invoked exclusively by the **Pause** button, which is rendered only when `hasChanges` is true (lines 799-808). There is no `useEffect` that persists innings/index on change.
+
+**What's wrong:** A test match is the longest-running scoring flow in the app, yet state is held only in React memory until the user manually taps Pause. A reload, tab crash, OS kill, or native app backgrounding before Pause loses the entire match — `loadQuickMatch` finds no `draftState` and the component reinitializes to 0/0. The task specifically calls out "persistence of a long match across reloads"; this flow has none beyond manual save.
+
+**User impact:** Hours of scoring can vanish on an accidental refresh or app suspension. For the test format specifically (no over limit, potentially very long), this is the highest-probability data-loss path.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Add a debounced auto-save effect that writes `draftState` to storage whenever `innings`/`currentInningsIndex`/`followOnEnforced` change (guarded by `hasChanges`), mirroring how a draft is built in `saveDraft`. Surface the existing storage-full warning on failure.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Draw awards 0 points while a tie awards 1 each — standings inconsistency
+
+**Evidence:** `cricketCalculations.js:289-307` (`applyMatchResult`): `winner === 'draw'` increments only `drawn` (no points); `winner === 'tie'` adds `points += 1` to both teams. `confirmDraw` sets `{ winner: 'draw' }` (`MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:377-380`).
+
+**What's wrong:** In real test/first-class points systems a draw normally awards points to both sides; here a draw is worth nothing while a numeric tie (extremely rare) is worth 1 each. Whether intentional or not, a tournament test match ending in a draw contributes 0 points and only bumps the `drawn` counter, which can distort standings. At minimum it is an undocumented asymmetry between two no-result-ish outcomes.
+
+**User impact:** Tournament standings after drawn test matches may not match user expectations; teams that draw are effectively unrewarded relative to a tie.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Decide and document the intended points model for draws; if draws should score points, add the award in `applyMatchResult`.
+
+**Confidence:** medium.
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] `getTestMatchResult` "tie" requires both sides to have played 2 innings, but the component can complete a match earlier — silent `{winner:null, desc:''}`
+
+**Evidence:** `cricketCalculations.js:227-236`: tie requires `t1Total === t2Total && t1Played >= 2 && t2Played >= 2`; otherwise if totals are exactly equal it falls through to `return { winner: null, desc: '' }`. `checkResult` (`MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:200`) only triggers completion on `battingTotal > bowlingTotal` (strict), and `advanceInnings` calls `getTestMatchResult` at `nextIndex >= 4` (line 225).
+
+**What's wrong:** If all four innings complete with **equal aggregate totals** but `countPlayedInnings` reads fewer than 2 played innings for a side (e.g. a side that was bowled out for a duck in an innings where `runs === 0 && !allOut && !declared` — `countPlayedInnings` filters on `allOut || declared || runs > 0`, so a 0-run innings that is somehow neither all-out nor declared is not counted), the function returns `{ winner: null, desc: '' }`. The match-complete UI then renders an empty description (`matchResult.desc` is `''`) with no winner name (lines 578-600). This is an edge case but yields a blank, confusing completion screen rather than "Match Tied" or a draw.
+
+**User impact:** Rare equal-totals end states can show a blank match-complete screen with no result text.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Have the component fall back to an explicit "Match Drawn"/"Match Tied" label when `getTestMatchResult` returns a null winner with empty desc after all innings are done; or make `countPlayedInnings` count any innings with `balls > 0`.
+
+**Confidence:** medium.
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Innings summary shows "/all" instead of "/10" for an all-out side
+
+**Evidence:** `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:623-624` and `722-723` render `${inn.runs}/${inn.allOut ? 'all' : inn.wickets}` — an all-out innings displays e.g. `301/all` rather than the conventional `301/10` (or `301 all out`).
+
+**What's wrong:** "/all" is non-standard cricket notation and looks like a glitch; conventional notation is `301` (all out) or `301/10`.
+
+**User impact:** Cosmetic confusion on the summary and match-complete tables.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Render `inn.runs` (implicitly all-out) or `${inn.runs}/${maxWickets}` for all-out innings; reserve `/N` for not-all-out.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Dead `#0066ff` blue hardcoded throughout — violates the green-monotone design token system
+
+**Evidence:** `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx:596` (result desc), `693` (context line), `704` (active innings tab), `786` (Declare border/text), `804` (Pause border/text), plus inline `#888`/`#eee`/`#bbb` greys.
+
+**What's wrong:** Per project design memory the app moved to a green monotone system (`var(--primary)`); `#0066ff` blue is explicitly "dead" and should be hunted down. This component still paints accents in the old blue and uses hardcoded hex greys instead of CSS tokens.
+
+**User impact:** Visual inconsistency with the rest of the Mono design (every accent here is the wrong colour).
+
+**Suggested direction:** Replace `#0066ff` with `var(--primary)` and hardcoded greys with the `--se-*`/token equivalents.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] No coverage for the test-match component or its key calc edges
+
+**Evidence:** Only `src/utils/cricketCalculations.test.js` exists; it never directly calls `getTestMatchResult` or `canEnforceFollowOn`, and there is no `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.test.jsx`. The 402-test green suite therefore does not exercise the 3rd-innings guard, follow-on enforcement, innings-victory labeling, or reload persistence — exactly where the critical/high bugs live.
+
+**What's wrong:** The most defect-dense logic (component gating) is untested, which is why the bugs above coexist with a green suite.
+
+**User impact:** Regressions in test-match scoring go undetected.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Add unit tests for `getTestMatchResult` (won by runs/wickets/innings, tie) and `canEnforceFollowOn`, plus component tests for the 3rd-innings transition, follow-on prompt (both leaders), and draft restore-on-reload.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
