+# Goals Scoring Engine Audit
+
+## Summary
+
+The goals engine spans two scorers: `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx` (tournament matches) and the goals branch of `MonoQuickMatch.jsx` (quick play). The quick-match path is well guarded — its no-draw check sits inside the single finalize function (`finalizeGoalScore`), so every save route (manual end + points auto-end) is covered. The tournament path is NOT: its no-draw guard lives only in the manual `saveMatch`, while the **timed auto-end** and **points auto-end** bypass it entirely and call `determineWinner`, which returns `'draw'` on equal scores with no `drawAllowed` check. Because basketball (and any no-draw goals sport) can run timed group AND knockout matches, a timed basketball match that expires tied auto-saves `winner: 'draw'` — a wrong result, and in a knockout it seeds the next round with a phantom `'draw'` "team", breaking bracket progression. Several setup gaps (mode toggle drops the timeLimit/target qualifier; start button doesn't re-gate) produce `NaN:NaN` timers and never-ending points matches.
+
+Severity tally: **Critical 1 · High 2 · Medium 3 · Low 3**
+
+---
+
+### [CRITICAL] Timed/points auto-end in tournament scorer ignores `drawAllowed`, saving `winner: 'draw'` for no-draw sports and breaking knockout brackets
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:137-153` (timed auto-end) and `:222-237` (points auto-end) both compute `winner: determineWinner(score1, score2, m)`. `determineWinner` (`:17-21`) returns `'draw'` whenever `s1 === s2`, with no `drawAllowed` check. The only no-draw guard is in the manual `saveMatch` (`:325` `if (!sportConfig.config.drawAllowed && score1 === score2)`), which the auto-end paths never touch. Basketball is `drawAllowed:false` (`src/models/sportRegistry.js:222`), and tournament setup lets any goals sport pick `timed` mode for both group matches (`src/designs/design1-mono/MonoTournamentSetup.jsx:1089`) and knockout matches (`:1433`).
+- **What's wrong:** When the timer reaches `timeLimit` with the score level, the timed effect auto-saves the match `status:'completed', winner:'draw'` without ever checking that the sport forbids draws. For a knockout match this is compounded: `updateKnockoutBracket` (`src/utils/knockoutManager.js:65-75`) seeds the final/3rd-place using `semi1.winner`/`semi2.winner` — now the literal string `'draw'` — and `getTournamentWinner` (`:118-126`) does `teams.find(t => t.id === final_.winner)` against `'draw'`, returning `null`.
+- **User impact:** A basketball (or any no-draw goals) tournament match that runs out of time tied is silently recorded as a "Draw" — a result that sport cannot have. In knockout play the bracket advances a non-existent `'draw'` team, corrupting final/3rd-place seeding and producing a null/blank tournament winner. The user gets no warning and cannot tell the result is invalid. (Standings points are unaffected because `standingsCalculator` recomputes from scores and no-ops on a tie for no-draw sports — `src/utils/standingsCalculator.js:139-148` — but the displayed match winner and bracket progression are wrong.)
+- **Suggested direction:** Make the auto-end paths honour `drawAllowed`. Either block auto-finish on a tie for no-draw sports (surface "cannot end tied / add the deciding score" like the quick-match `finalizeGoalScore` does) or route both auto-ends through a shared finalize helper that enforces the no-draw rule, and never persist `winner:'draw'` when `drawAllowed:false`.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Selecting "By time" / "By points" without choosing a preset starts a match with no `timeLimit`/`target`
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1895` — the mode toggle calls `setFormat({ mode: opt.key })`, dropping any previously-set `timeLimit`/`target`. The preset pickers (`:1918` timed, `:1938` points) are the only thing that re-adds the qualifier, and they are rendered separately/optionally. `startMatch` (`:728-729`) and the Start button (`:2158` `disabled={!teamNamesReady}`) gate ONLY on team names — never on a chosen timeLimit/target.
+- **What's wrong:** A user can tap "By time" (or "By points") and start without picking a duration/target. `format.timeLimit`/`format.target` is then `undefined`.
+- **User impact (timed):** `remainingSeconds = Math.max(0, timeLimit - timer.elapsed)` with `timeLimit === undefined` → `Math.max(0, NaN)` → `NaN`; `timerDisplay = formatCountdown(NaN)` (`:2175-2177`, `:547-551`) renders **"NaN:NaN"** on the scoreboard, and `isTimeUp` never becomes true so the match cannot resolve by time. **User impact (points):** the badge/footer show "First to undefined" (`:2385`, `:2494`) and the auto-end `if (isPointsMode && format.target ...)` (`:1195`) never fires, so the match never ends on its own. Defaults only protect the initial "Standard" state (`src/utils/sportDefaults.js:71-111`); the bug appears the moment the user actively switches mode.
+- **Suggested direction:** Default a `timeLimit`/`target` when the mode is selected (e.g. first preset), or disable Start until one is chosen, or guard `formatCountdown`/`remainingSeconds` against a missing limit.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] Same "no preset" gap exists in tournament setup (group + knockout) for timed/points goals formats
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoTournamentSetup.jsx:1089` and `:1433` seed a sensible default `timeLimit` when "By time" is first tapped, but the points toggles (`:1096`, `:1440`) seed `target: sportConfig.config.pointPresets?.[0] || 10` — good — yet the per-tab handlers can still leave `format` in a `{ mode }`-only shape via the free→back navigation, and the knockout "By time" path depends on `sportConfig.config.timePresets?.[0]`. Cross-referenced with `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:388` `timeLimit = isTimedMode ? effectiveFormat.timeLimit : null` and `:389` `remainingSeconds` — a missing `timeLimit` yields the same `NaN` countdown in the tournament scorer.
+- **What's wrong:** The tournament timed-format selection relies on `timePresets[0]` existing; any goals sport whose `timePresets` were empty, or a format object that reaches the scorer with `mode:'timed'` but no `timeLimit`, renders a `NaN:NaN` clock and a match that never auto-ends. The same start flow has no validation that a timed format carries a `timeLimit`.
+- **User impact:** A tournament goals match can be launched with a broken countdown that never expires, requiring manual end and showing garbage time on screen.
+- **Suggested direction:** Validate `format.timeLimit`/`format.target` before persisting the tournament; guard the scorer's countdown helpers against an undefined limit.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Quick-match timed mode: scoring continues after expiry while the clock reads "Time's up!" and never resolves
+
+- **Evidence:** In `MonoQuickMatch.jsx`, `isTimeUp` (`:543`) is used ONLY to color the timer (`:2178`) and swap the label to "Time's up!" (`:2378`). There is no timed auto-end effect and `addGoal` (`:1180-1200`) has no `isTimeUp` guard; the score buttons (`:2459-2480`, `:2399/:2429`) are never disabled on time-up. By contrast the tournament scorer DOES both: it blocks scoring after expiry (`MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:183-185`) and auto-ends (`:137-163`), and disables its buttons (`:480/:491/:522/:533`).
+- **What's wrong:** Once the timer hits the limit, the countdown is pinned at "Time's up!" but the user can keep adding goals indefinitely and nothing ends the match — a self-contradictory state that diverges from the tournament scorer's behavior.
+- **User impact:** Confusing UX: the app says time is up yet still accepts scoring, and the only way to finish is manual "End Match". Two scorers for the same sport behave differently.
+- **Suggested direction:** Decide on one rule. Either auto-end (mirror the tournament scorer) or, if "play continues until you end it" is intended, change the label to something like "Full time — end the match" and keep accepting scores deliberately; don't show a frozen "Time's up!" beside live scoring.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Points auto-end fires inside a state-updater closure and a 300 ms `setTimeout`, risking a double finalize / double navigate
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:222-247` — on reaching `target`, `addScore` schedules `setTimeout(() => { ...save... navigate(...) }, 300)` but does NOT disable further scoring, clear a guard, or cancel a prior timeout. The 150 ms click debounce (`:188-190`) is shorter than the 300 ms navigation delay, so a second qualifying click can land and schedule a second save+navigate. The same shape exists in the timed auto-end (`:142-163`).
+- **What's wrong:** Between the target being reached and the delayed navigation, the UI is still interactive; a second tap (or a quick-button worth several points) can enqueue a second `updateMatchInTournament` + `navigate`, double-writing the match and double-navigating.
+- **User impact:** Rare but real: a fast second tap right at match point can re-save the tournament (last-write-wins, possibly with a different score) and fire navigation twice. Low frequency, but a correctness/race hazard at the exact end-of-match boundary.
+- **Suggested direction:** Set an `isFinishing` ref/flag the instant target is reached, gate `addScore`/auto-end on it, and clear any pending `autoFinishTimeoutRef` before scheduling a new one (the timed path already uses `autoFinishTimeoutRef`; the points path does not).
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] Tournament scorer cannot save a finished match with a 0–0 / no-score scenario distinctly, and confetti gating differs from save gating
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:331` gates celebration on `if (score1 !== 0 || score2 !== 0)`, but `saveMatch` (`:336-352`) saves regardless. For a `drawAllowed:false` sport at 0–0, the no-draw guard (`:325`) correctly blocks the save with a warning — good — but for a draw-allowed sport (football) a deliberate 0–0 saves silently with no celebration, while every other scoreline celebrates. Cross-checked: quick match has the same celebration-vs-save split via `finalizeGoalScore`.
+- **What's wrong:** Minor inconsistency: a legitimate 0–0 football result saves but is treated as "nothing happened" (no confetti/haptic), which can read as if the Save didn't register.
+- **User impact:** A real 0–0 draw looks like a no-op on save; users may tap Save again, unsure it worked.
+- **Suggested direction:** Either always confirm the save visually (toast) regardless of score, or keep confetti but add a neutral "Saved" acknowledgement for 0–0.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Rugby has no standalone "Conversion (2)" button; a try-then-conversion needs two corrections
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/models/sportRegistry.js:394-399` rugby `quickButtons` = Penalty(3), Try(5), Try+Conv(7), Drop(3). There is no `+2` conversion. The generic `adjustGoalScore` only does ±1 (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1213-1232`).
+- **What's wrong:** A try (5) followed later by a successful conversion can't be entered as "+2"; the scorer must either pre-guess "Try+Conv (7)" or add the 2 via two `+1` corrections.
+- **User impact:** Awkward real-time scoring for the common "try now, kick the conversion a moment later" sequence.
+- **Suggested direction:** Add a `Conversion (2)` quick button for rugby.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] `addScore`/`addGoal` debounce can swallow legitimately fast scoring on multi-value buttons
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:188-190` and `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1181-1183` both reject any click within 150 ms of the previous one. With basketball/rugby quick buttons (distinct +1/+2/+3 or penalty/try), a fast scorer logging two quick events in under 150 ms loses the second.
+- **What's wrong:** The debounce is a blunt time gate, not a per-button or accidental-double-tap guard; rapid but intentional taps are dropped.
+- **User impact:** Occasional missed points during fast play; the user sees the score not increment and may re-tap, compounding confusion.
+- **Suggested direction:** Lower the window, or only debounce identical repeated taps on the same target, rather than all scoring globally.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] Hardcoded legacy blue accent (`#0066ff`) in the tournament goals scorer contradicts the green design system
+
+- **Evidence:** `MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:426-427` (Swap button active), `:584` (Save Draft button). Project design tokens mandate the field-green accent and explicitly call the old `#0066ff` blue "dead" (per the Mono design system).
+- **What's wrong:** Stale blue accent leaks into the active scoring screen.
+- **User impact:** Visual inconsistency with the rest of the app on the Swap/Save-Draft controls.
+- **Suggested direction:** Replace `#0066ff` with `var(--primary)`.
+- **Confidence:** high
