+# Tennis Scoring Engine
+
+## Summary
+
+The tennis point/game/set/match math in `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx` is, for the core ladder, **correct**: the 0/15/30/40 ladder, deuce/advantage transitions, game win, 7-5 win-by-2 sets, 6-6 tiebreak entry, and tiebreak win-by-2 all reproduce faithfully in a standalone replay of the engine helpers. Match outcome and the sets tally are computed from tiebreak points where applicable, so **the saved winner is always correct**. The defects are concentrated in display/state consistency at boundary moments and in test coverage: a tiebreak-won set never updates its game score to 7-6 (it stays 6-6 in the live row and in stored history), the scorer advances into a phantom empty set the instant a best-of-N match clinches before the final set, and — most importantly for reconciling the green unit suite — the one tennis util that *is* unit-tested (`tennisScoring.js`) is **dead code never imported by the running scorer**, so the live deuce/tiebreak/advance logic has zero unit tests. There is no serve indication and no deciding-set variant.
+
+Severity tally: critical 0 · high 2 · medium 2 · low 4.
+
+### [HIGH] Tiebreak-won set never updates games to 7-6 (live row and stored history show 6-6)
+
+**File:line evidence:** `src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:155-168` (`processTiebreakPoint`) increments only `tiebreakPoints` and sets `completed = true`; it never increments the winner's `games` from 6 to 7. The only places `games` is incremented are `applyDeuceLogic` (116, 124) and `applyRegularGameLogic` (140, 146), both on the regular-game path. The 6-6→tiebreak transition (186-190) flips `isTiebreak` but never adds the 13th game.
+
+**What's wrong:** A real tennis set won in a tiebreak ends 7-6. Here both teams' `games` stay at 6. Verified by replay: a tiebreak won 7-5 leaves `games1=6, games2=6, completed=true`.
+
+**User impact:** Two sites.
+1. Live "Match Score" row renders `6-6 (7-5)` (`MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:708-710`) instead of the standard `7-6`.
+2. Quick-match history persists the wrong set score: `mapTennisSetsForQuickHistory` copies `score1=games1=6, score2=games2=6` (`src/utils/tennisQuickMatch.js:12-15`), so the stored row has `score1 === score2` — a completed set looks like a 6-6 draw in history even though one player clearly won it. Any consumer that infers the set winner from `score1`/`games1` (rather than `tiebreakPoints`) would misread it.
+
+**Outcome unaffected:** the match winner and sets tally are NOT wrong — `countSetsWon` (87-98) and `countTennisQuickSetsWon` (`tennisQuickMatch.js:23-37`) break the per-set tie using `tiebreakPoints`, and the saved `winner` (`MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:519-520`, `tennisQuickMatch.js:60-64`) derives from those set counts. So this is a persistent display/stored-score correctness bug, not a result bug.
+
+**Suggested direction:** In `processTiebreakPoint`, when the tiebreak completes, increment the winning side's `games` (6→7) before marking `completed`. Then `countSetsWon`'s `games1 > games2` path also becomes self-consistent and the history row stops showing a phantom draw.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+### [HIGH] Match-clinch advances `currentSet` into a phantom empty set (Bo3/Bo5 decided early)
+
+**File:line evidence:** `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:357-359` — `advanceFn` advances `currentSet` whenever `setIdx < totalSets - 1`, with no check for whether the match just ended. `isMatchComplete` (334-339) becomes true the moment a side reaches `ceil(sets.length/2)` set wins, but the scorer has already moved `currentSet` to the next (empty) slot.
+
+**What's wrong:** In a best-of-3 where a player wins the first two sets, clinching set index 1 fires `advanceFn(1, 3)`; `1 < 2` so `currentSet` becomes 2, an empty `makeBlankSet()`. Verified by replay: after the clinching set, `currentSet = 2`, `sets[2] = {games:0/0, points:0/0}`.
+
+**User impact:** The main score pad then reads from `currentSetData = sets[2]` (556) and shows **0 / 0, Games: 0** for an already-won match, the badge shows **"Set 3 of 3"** (618) even though "Match Complete" is also displayed (620-622), and because `sets[2].completed` is false, `canScoreCurrentSet` (569) is true — the score pads render as live (blue border, full opacity, 635-688) and look clickable. Clicking is silently swallowed by the `if (isMatchComplete) return;` guard in `addPoint` (348), so the user taps an enabled-looking pad and nothing happens. Confusing end-of-match state. The saved result is still correct (only `completed` sets count toward `winner`).
+
+**Suggested direction:** In `advanceFn` (or right after a set completes), stop advancing once `isMatchComplete` would be true; keep `currentSet` on the decider and let the "Match Complete" UI own the screen. The dead-looking-but-enabled pad is a direct consequence of this same advance — fixing the advance fixes both.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+### [MEDIUM] Unit-tested tennis util is dead code; the live engine has no unit tests
+
+**File:line evidence:** `src/utils/tennisScoring.js:1-11` (`formatTennisPointScore`) is imported only by `src/utils/tennisScoring.test.js:2` (verified by repo-wide grep — no production file imports it). The live scorer formats points with its own `pointToDisplay` (32-35) + `computeScoreDisplay` (203-216) and never calls `formatTennisPointScore`. Tennis quick matches route to `MonoTennisLiveScore` via `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:793` (`/${sport}/quick/live/:id`); tournament tennis routes via `MonoTournamentLiveScore.jsx:31`. Neither path touches `tennisScoring.js` or `MonoSetsLiveScore`'s tennis branch.
+
+**What's wrong:** The green suite's tennis coverage (`tennisScoring.test.js`, 6 assertions) exercises a formatter that does not run in the app. The actual engine — deuce/advantage, game completion, 6-6 tiebreak entry, tiebreak win-by-2, set/match completion, `countSetsWon`, the set-advance logic — has **no** unit tests, because the helpers live inside the component module and are not exported. This is why a "scoring is wrong" finding can coexist with an all-green suite: the suite isn't testing the engine.
+
+**User impact:** Regressions in the real tennis engine (e.g. the two HIGH bugs above) ship undetected by CI. Maintenance risk.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Either delete `tennisScoring.js` (and its test) as dead code, or wire it into the live display so the tested code is the running code. Independently, extract the engine helpers (`processRegularPoint`, `applyDeuceLogic`, `processTiebreakPoint`, `isSetComplete`, `countSetsWon`, the `advanceFn` policy) into a testable module and add unit tests covering deuce/advantage, 7-5 win-by-2, 6-6 tiebreak completion with the games update, best-of-3/5 early clinch, and tiebreak-set `countSetsWon`.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+### [MEDIUM] No serve / server indication anywhere in the tennis scorer
+
+**File:line evidence:** `grep -i serv` over `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx` returns no matches; `makeBlankSet` (56-67) has no `server`/`serving` field, and there is no server-alternation logic (server changes every game, and every point in a tiebreak after the first). The registry even notes `serviceRotation: null` for tennis (`src/models/sportRegistry.js:112`).
+
+**What's wrong:** The scorer cannot show who is serving, which is core tennis context a scorekeeper expects (and which the audit brief lists under "service indication").
+
+**User impact:** Users tracking a real match have no on-screen serve indicator and the app never prompts the service change at game/tiebreak boundaries. Missing feature, not a math error.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Add an optional `server` field to the set model, toggle it on each game win (and per-point in tiebreak per the every-2-points-after-first rule if precision is wanted), and render a serve dot next to the serving side.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+### [LOW] `computeScoreDisplay(currentSetData)` is not null-guarded while the adjacent read is
+
+**File:line evidence:** `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:557` guards `currentSetData?.isTiebreak`, but line 560 calls `computeScoreDisplay(currentSetData)` and `computeScoreDisplay` (203) dereferences `setData.isTiebreak` with no guard. `currentSet` can be `-1` if a loaded `draftSets` array is empty, because `findIndex(s => !s.completed)` returns `-1` (296-300; same pattern at 222-223).
+
+**What's wrong:** If `sets` is empty or `currentSet` is `-1`, `currentSetData` is `undefined` and `computeScoreDisplay(undefined)` throws, crashing the scorer (white screen).
+
+**User impact:** Crash — but only reachable via a corrupted/tampered draft (empty `sets` with a draft present). The normal flow always writes a non-empty `sets` array (`buildInitialSets` ≥ format.sets), so reachability is low.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Guard `currentSetData` (early-return the "Match not found" / loading branch) when it is undefined, matching the `?.` already used one line above.
+
+**Confidence:** low.
+
+### [LOW] In-progress tiebreak shows "6-6" in the Match Score row with no live tiebreak points
+
+**File:line evidence:** `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:708-710` — the row appends tiebreak points only when `set.isTiebreak && set.completed`. During a live tiebreak (`isTiebreak` true, `completed` false) the row renders just `${games1}-${games2}` → `6-6`, while the main score pad shows the running tiebreak points.
+
+**What's wrong:** The Match Score list and the main pad disagree during a live tiebreak — the list omits the tiebreak progress.
+
+**User impact:** Minor: the per-set list doesn't reflect live tiebreak progress; the main pad does. Display-only.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Show `${games1}-${games2} (${tiebreakPoints1}-${tiebreakPoints2})` whenever `isTiebreak` is true, not only when completed.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+### [LOW] Tiebreak badge drops the "Set N of M" context
+
+**File:line evidence:** `MonoTennisLiveScore.jsx:615-619` and the `<output>` live region 627-630 — when `isTiebreakMode` the badge shows only `Tiebreak` and the `Set N of M` text is dropped, including for screen-reader users.
+
+**What's wrong:** During a tiebreak the user (and assistive tech) loses the "which set" indicator.
+
+**User impact:** Minor loss of context during the most tense phase of a set; affects accessibility output too.
+
+**Suggested direction:** Render e.g. `Set N of M · Tiebreak` so the set number is retained in both the badge and the live region.
+
+**Confidence:** high.
+
+### [LOW] No configurable deciding-set variant (informational, not a defect)
+
+**File:line evidence:** `isSetComplete` (74-84) and the 6-6 transition (186-190) apply an identical 7-point tiebreak at 6-6 to **every** set, including the decider. There is no branch for a final-set super-tiebreak (first to 10) or an advantage final set.
+
+**What's wrong:** Nothing is incorrect — a standard 7-point tiebreak at 6-6 in the deciding set is correct modern recreational behavior. The only thing absent is the *optional* deciding-set variant some formats use (10-point super-tiebreak / advantage set).
+
+**User impact:** Users who want to score a super-tiebreak deciding set cannot; standard scoring is fine.
+
+**Suggested direction:** If desired, add a format option for a 10-point deciding-set tiebreak (and/or advantage final set) and branch `isSetComplete` on the final set index.
+
+**Confidence:** low.
