+# Cricket — Per-Sport Audit
+
+## Summary
+
+Cricket is the only `custom-cricket` engine sport. Its real scoring path lives in `cricketCalculations.js` (CRICKET_FORMATS) and the dedicated `isCricket` branch of `MonoQuickMatch.jsx`, plus `MonoCricketTestLiveScore.jsx` and `MonoCricketTournament.jsx`. The setup → scorer → save → history → standings flow is coherent and the format model (T20/T10/ODI/Test/Gully/Custom, overs, players, powerplay, free-hit, declaration/follow-on) is rich and largely correct. The main problems are in the **`sportRegistry.js` cricket `config` block, which is almost entirely dead/decorative**: cricket reads none of it (`scoringUnit`, `pointIncrement`, `winPoints`, `lossPoints`, `quickButtons`), and `standingsColumns` does not match the table the cricket tournament actually renders. There is also a unit test that locks in this dead config, giving false confidence.
+
+Severity tally: critical 0, high 2, medium 3, low 3.
+
+---
+
+### [HIGH] cricket-standings-columns-mismatch — `standingsColumns` declares NR, omits T, and is never used by the cricket table
+
+- Evidence: `src/models/sportRegistry.js:442` declares `standingsColumns: ['P','W','L','NR','NRR','Pts']`. The actual cricket points table renders a hardcoded header `# Team P W L T Pts NRR` at `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoCricketTournament.jsx:529-536` and body at `:544-554` (renders `played, won, lost, tied, points, nrr`). The cricket table never imports or reads `standingsColumns` (grep: only `GenericGoalsTournament.jsx:370` and `GenericSetsTournament.jsx:431` consume `standingsColumns`, both non-cricket).
+- What's wrong: The declared columns are wrong on two counts — they list `NR` (No Result), which is never displayed (and never even incremented; see cricket-noresult-dead below), and they omit `T` (Tied), which the table actually shows. Because the cricket tournament hardcodes its own header, the registry value is dead and misleading to any future code/agent that trusts it.
+- User impact: None directly visible today (cricket table works), but it is a latent defect: any shared standings renderer that starts honoring `standingsColumns` for cricket would draw the wrong columns (missing Tied, phantom NR). It also misrepresents cricket's standings contract in the single source of truth.
+- Suggested direction: Either make the cricket tournament consume `standingsColumns`, or fix the registry to `['P','W','L','T','Pts','NRR']` to match the real table. Decide one source of truth.
+- Confidence: high.
+
+### [HIGH] cricket-config-block-dead — entire cricket `config` (scoringUnit/pointIncrement/winPoints/lossPoints/quickButtons) is never read by the cricket flow
+
+- Evidence: `src/models/sportRegistry.js:426-441` defines `config: { scoringUnit:'run', pointIncrement:1, drawAllowed:false, winPoints:2, lossPoints:0, quickButtons:[…,{label:'W',value:0,isWicket:true}] }`. The cricket scoring branch (`isCricket`, `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:2191-2360`) renders run buttons from `CRICKET_RUN_VALUES` (`:2310`) via `addRuns`/`addWicket`/`addExtra`, not `quickButtons`. The `quickButtons` rendering block (`:2456-2480`) lives in the non-cricket goals branch and calls `addGoal` — unreachable for cricket. Standings points are hardcoded (`+2` win / `+1` tie) in `src/utils/cricketCalculations.js:295,299,303,305`, not read from `winPoints`. `scoringUnit`/`pointIncrement` are only consumed by sets/goals code paths (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:250,544,1942`).
+- What's wrong: The config block looks authoritative but is decorative for cricket. The `quickButtons` `W` (`value:0, isWicket:true`) in particular implies the registry drives the wicket button; it does not.
+- User impact: No runtime impact today, but it is a correctness trap: editing `winPoints`, `quickButtons`, or `scoringUnit` for cricket changes nothing, which will silently waste any future tuning and mislead maintainers.
+- Suggested direction: Either delete the unused cricket config fields (keep only what is read) or wire the cricket engine to read them (e.g. points from `winPoints`, run buttons from `quickButtons`). At minimum add a comment that cricket ignores this block.
+- Confidence: high.
+
+---
+
+### [MEDIUM] cricket-quickbuttons-test-false-confidence — unit test asserts on dead `quickButtons.isWicket` config
+
+- Evidence: `src/models/sportRegistry.test.js:176-180` asserts `SPORT_REGISTRY.cricket.config.quickButtons.find(b => b.isWicket)` exists with `label==='W'`. As shown above, this array is never rendered by the cricket UI (`CRICKET_RUN_VALUES` is used instead).
+- What's wrong: A green test guards config that has no runtime effect, creating false confidence that "the cricket wicket button is covered." It also makes the dead `quickButtons` array harder to remove (the test would need updating).
+- User impact: Indirect — masks the fact that the real wicket button (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:2332`) has no registry-driven test.
+- Suggested direction: Repoint the test at the real source (`CRICKET_RUN_VALUES` / the rendered Wicket control), or remove the assertion when the dead config is removed.
+- Confidence: high.
+
+### [MEDIUM] cricket-noresult-dead — `noResult` is tracked but never incremented and never shown; matches the phantom NR column
+
+- Evidence: `src/utils/cricketCalculations.js:313` initializes `noResult: 0` on every table row, but no code path ever increments it (`applyMatchResult` at `:288-307` only handles draw/win/win/tie). The registry advertises an `NR` column (`sportRegistry.js:442`) that is never rendered. There is no UI to mark a cricket match abandoned / no-result.
+- What's wrong: No-result is a standard cricket outcome (rain, abandoned) worth 1 point each in most league formats; here it is a vestigial field with no producer and no display.
+- User impact: Users cannot record a washed-out / no-result match; such a match can only be saved as a real result, tie, or left incomplete, skewing standings.
+- Suggested direction: Either implement no-result (a "No Result" end-match option that increments `noResult` and awards points), or remove the dead field and the NR column claim.
+- Confidence: high.
+
+### [MEDIUM] cricket-draw-zero-points — a drawn Test/match awards 0 points to both teams
+
+- Evidence: `src/utils/cricketCalculations.js:288-293` — when `winner === 'draw'`, both teams get `drawn++` but **no `points +=`**. Test matches resolve to `'draw'` when neither side is bowled out / target not chased (`getMatchWinner` / `getTestMatchResult`, `:168-234`).
+- What's wrong: In essentially every points-based cricket competition a drawn (not tied) match awards points to both teams (e.g. ICC WTC, Ranji, county systems). Awarding 0/0 for a hard-fought draw is wrong for standings.
+- User impact: Tournament standings under-reward drawn Test matches; a team that draws every game shows 0 points despite never losing. (Note: this is in the shared standings calculator, near the engine boundary — flagging for the engine owner, but it is cricket-specific because only cricket produces draws via `getMatchWinner`.)
+- Suggested direction: Award configurable draw points (commonly 1 each) in `applyMatchResult` for `winner === 'draw'`.
+- Confidence: high.
+
+---
+
+### [LOW] cricket-custom-overs-stale — invalid/blank custom-overs input keeps the previous `format.overs` silently
+
+- Evidence: `src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1602-1606` — `onChange` sets the displayed `customOvers` string unconditionally but only updates `format.overs` when `v >= 1 && v <= 50`. Clearing the field or typing `0`/`>50` leaves `format.overs` at its prior value while the input shows the (invalid/empty) text.
+- What's wrong: The visible input and the actual match overs can diverge with no error feedback. E.g. pick "20 ov" → Custom → clear field → input is empty but match still runs 20 overs.
+- User impact: Minor confusion; user may believe overs are unset/different from what the match uses. No data loss (a valid number must have been entered at some point or the preset default applies).
+- Suggested direction: On invalid/empty input, either reset `format.overs` to a sentinel and disable Start, or show inline validation.
+- Confidence: high.
+
+### [LOW] cricket-odi-powerplay-mislabel — ODI "PP2" (overs 11-40) is labeled a powerplay though it is the non-restricted middle phase
+
+- Evidence: `src/utils/cricketCalculations.js:25-29` defines ODI powerplays as `PP1 1-10`, `PP2 11-40`, `PP3 41-50`. The scoring UI shows "PP2 (Overs 11-40)" as an active powerplay (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:2274-2278` via `getPowerplayPhase`).
+- What's wrong: In real ODIs only overs 1-10 (PP1) and 41-50 (PP3) are fielding-restricted powerplays; 11-40 is the standard middle phase (4 fielders out), not a powerplay. Labeling it "PP2" as a powerplay phase is inaccurate.
+- User impact: Cosmetic/educational only — the indicator always shows a "powerplay" phase across the whole ODI innings, which is misleading to cricket-literate users. No scoring effect (phases are display-only).
+- Suggested direction: Either drop the 11-40 phase or relabel it "Middle overs (no powerplay)".
+- Confidence: medium.
+
+### [LOW] cricket-features-bullet-overreach — marketing bullets list capabilities that are partial or unrendered
+
+- Evidence: `src/models/sportRegistry.js:443-449` lists `'NRR points table'`, `'Powerplay & free-hit indicators'`, `'Declaration & follow-on (Test)'`. NRR table is real (`MonoCricketTournament.jsx`). However the related `standingsColumns` advertises an `NR` column that is never shown (see cricket-standings-columns-mismatch), and the ODI powerplay indicator is mislabeled (see above). Free-hit and follow-on/declaration exist in the format model.
+- What's wrong: The feature list is broadly accurate but inherits the NR-column and ODI-powerplay inaccuracies, and is the marketing-facing summary users see on the sport picker.
+- User impact: Low — slight overstatement; the headline features do exist.
+- Suggested direction: Keep the bullets but resolve the underlying NR/powerplay issues so the claims are fully truthful.
+- Confidence: medium.
+
+---
+
+## Non-issues verified (explicitly OK)
+
+- Icon `🏏` (`sportRegistry.js:422`) is unique — no duplication with any other sport.
+- `desc: 'Overs, wickets, NRR'` (`:424`) accurately reflects the cricket UI.
+- Setup flow works end-to-end: `/play` → cricket card → `/cricket/quick` format cards (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1422-1459`) → overs/players/match-type controls (`:1556-1699`) → Test (`totalInnings===4`) routes to `/cricket/quick/test-match/:id` (`:756-774`); limited-overs runs the `isCricket` scoring branch and saves to history.
+- Custom-overs numeric bounds (1-50) match ODI's max; OVERS_PRESETS (2/5/10/20/50) are realistic.
+- Chase-end logic (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:830-835`): ends on `runs > target` (strictly exceeding the first-innings total), which is the correct win condition; equality correctly remains a potential tie resolved at over/wicket exhaustion.
