+# Kabaddi
+
+## Summary
+
+The kabaddi config (`src/models/sportRegistry.js:344-378`) is largely correct: it uses the `goals` engine, time presets are arithmetically right (2×20 min = Pro Kabaddi League format), `desc` uses accurate kabaddi terminology, and the draw/standings wiring functions because the standings calculator supplies a sensible `drawPoints` default. The setup → scorer → save → history flow works end to end for kabaddi via the shared goals path. The real issues are config-hygiene and terminology problems rather than crashes: a dead `maxPlayers: 7` field that nothing reads, an implicit (undeclared) `drawPoints` that relies on a downstream default, two functionally-identical quick buttons with misleading "+2" labels, and a "by points" target mode that does not match how kabaddi is actually won.
+
+Severity tally: critical 0, high 0, medium 3, low 3.
+
+### [MEDIUM] `drawPoints` is undeclared despite `drawAllowed: true`
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/models/sportRegistry.js:351-371` — kabaddi config defines `drawAllowed: true`, `winPoints: 2`, `lossPoints: 0`, and `standingsColumns` includes a `'D'` column, but there is **no `drawPoints` key**. Compare rugby at `src/models/sportRegistry.js:391-393`, which explicitly sets `drawPoints: 2`. The standings calculator destructures `const { winPoints = 2, drawPoints = 1, lossPoints = 0, drawAllowed = false } = config;` at `src/utils/standingsCalculator.js:96`.
+- **What's wrong:** A drawn kabaddi match awards each team `drawPoints` = the **default `1`** because the config omits the key. The behavior happens to be reasonable (win 2 / draw 1 / loss 0), but it is implicit and inconsistent with how every other draw-allowing goals sport behaves: football/hockey/handball/futsal all declare `drawPoints: 1` explicitly. The value is correct only by accident of the default.
+- **User impact:** Low-visibility today, but fragile — if the calculator default ever changes, kabaddi standings silently break with no config signal. Also an inconsistency reviewers will trip over.
+- **Suggested direction:** Add `drawPoints: 1` to the kabaddi config so the standings math is explicit and matches the other draw-allowing sports.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+### [MEDIUM] Two quick buttons are functionally identical with misleading labels
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/models/sportRegistry.js:357-361` — quickButtons are `{ '+1', 1 }`, `{ 'Super Tackle (+2)', 2 }`, `{ 'All Out (+2)', 2 }`. The goals scorer simply does `addGoal(team, btn.value)` (`src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:2462,2474`) and the tournament scorer does `addScore(...btn.value)` (`src/designs/design1-mono/scoring/MonoGoalsLiveScore.jsx:477,520`). Neither button records *what kind* of point it was — both "Super Tackle" and "All Out" just add 2 and are indistinguishable.
+- **What's wrong:** The two distinct labels imply distinct tracking/semantics, but they are the same `+2` action. Worse, the labels are factually loose for kabaddi: a "Super Tackle" is worth **+2** (correct), but an "All Out" awards **+2 bonus on top of the points already scored for emptying the opposing side** — modeling it as a flat "+2" button invites the scorer to under-count. There is no plain raid/tackle distinction either.
+- **User impact:** A user scoring an all-out who taps only the "All Out (+2)" button records 2 points when the real swing is usually larger; the duplicate labels also look like a bug ("why are there two +2 buttons?").
+- **Suggested direction:** Either collapse to `+1` / `+2` generic buttons, or keep descriptive labels but document that they are point-value shortcuts only; consider whether "All Out" should be a larger or variable value.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+### [MEDIUM] "By points" target mode does not reflect how kabaddi is won
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/models/sportRegistry.js:369` — `pointPresets: [20, 30, 40, 50]`. The goals setup offers a "By points / First to N" mode for all goals sports (`src/designs/design1-mono/MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1929-1947`), and `addGoal` auto-ends the match when `newS1 >= format.target || newS2 >= format.target` (`MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1195-1199`).
+- **What's wrong:** Real kabaddi is decided by time (2×20 min) and points-difference, never by a first-to-N race. Offering "First to 20 points" as a preset misrepresents the sport. It is a generic casual fallback shared by every goals sport, so it is not kabaddi-specific breakage — but the presets are presented without any "casual" caveat.
+- **User impact:** Minor; a user could pick a mode that ends the match in a way that does not match a real kabaddi game. The `+2` quick buttons also overshoot the target (e.g. 19→21 on a Super Tackle), which is handled by the `>=` check but means the final score can exceed the stated target.
+- **Suggested direction:** Acceptable to keep as a casual option; if precision matters, hide "by points" for time-based sports like kabaddi, or label it clearly as a house-rule mode.
+- **Confidence:** medium
+
+### [LOW] `maxPlayers: 7` is dead config (never read)
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/models/sportRegistry.js:362` defines `maxPlayers: 7`. A repo-wide search shows the only other `maxPlayers` references are local cricket-only variables `const maxPlayers = format.solo ? 10 : 11;` in `MonoQuickMatch.jsx:1672` and `MonoTournamentSetup.jsx:957` — neither reads `sportConfig.config.maxPlayers`. No code path consumes the kabaddi field.
+- **What's wrong:** The field looks meaningful (kabaddi is 7-a-side) but has zero effect on setup, scoring, or display.
+- **User impact:** None functionally; it is misleading to future maintainers who may assume team size is enforced.
+- **Suggested direction:** Either wire it into a "players per side" control for goals sports or remove it to avoid implying functionality that does not exist.
+- **Confidence:** high
+
+### [LOW] `features` marketing bullets are never rendered
+
+- **Evidence:** `src/models/sportRegistry.js:372-377` lists feature bullets ("Raid & tackle points", "All-out bonus (2pts)", etc.). A repo-wide search for `.features` finds only `src/models/sportRegistry.test.js:58-59`, which asserts the array is non-empty — no component renders it.
+- **What's wrong:** The bullets are dead metadata. They are also slightly inconsistent with the config (they advertise "Super tackle bonus" and "All-out bonus (2pts)" as if separately tracked, but see the duplicate-button finding above — they are not).
+- **User impact:** None (not user-visible). Only matters for internal consistency and any future surface that decides to render features.
